        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ad2da2c0-9466-11e8-aaea-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ad2da2c0-9466-11e8-aaea-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2011-05-23T04:00:12</review:date>
        <review:description>As the title implies, this game ruined the genre of space sims for me.  No game has ever come close to matching the awe inspiring battles fought in this game.  The dog fighting is intense, and the capital ships are truly frightening presences.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>ad2da2c0-9466-11e8-aaea-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>This game ruined space sims for me</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ad3c5bb8-9215-11ea-8e6a-fa163e93294d -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ad3c5bb8-9215-11ea-8e6a-fa163e93294d">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-05-09T19:53:58</review:date>
        <review:description>Aside from a controller scheme that could use some tweaking, the gameplay is solid, find the best weapons for you and your workers to overthrow the various mechanisms of the oppressive bourgeoisie.
The 16bit pixel look isn&apos;t  usually a look I enjoy, but here it adds to the visual appeal of the mayhem giving things a decidedly lo-fi feel. The soundtrack is uptempo synth, which fits the gameplay, in your face, but not overwhelming. 

Obviously taking things to ridiculous levels, the themes of workers rights and capitalist exploration shouldn&apos;t be as rare and seemingly shocking as such a game makes them seem.

Wife and I will be putting a lot of time into smashing the pixelated machinery  of the ruling class and I thank Pixel Pushers Union 512 for that :)
</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">119</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>ad3c5bb8-9215-11ea-8e6a-fa163e93294d</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Pixel against the machine</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">70</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


