    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9372b72-4229-11eb-bd9a-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9372b72-4229-11eb-bd9a-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-12-19T20:40:53</review:date>
        <review:description>“Tonight We Riot” is a perfect mixture of “Tooth and Tail” and “Nuclear Throne.” If you enjoyed either of those games, you will enjoy this one. “Tonight We Riot” encapsulates you with a vivid atmosphere that is supported greatly by a spartan but excellent soundtrack, and I felt the difficult was perfect for me, considering I seldom play games of this genre. The story is interesting and original, and despite its seemingly crass nature, the game presents mature notions to consider that you will mull over even after you have stopped playing it.

“Tonight We Riot” has some issues, however. The game is short, and it does not have as much replay value as either of its aforementioned competitors. Its controls can be cumbersome at times too. Fighting enemies’ drones is frustrating. It is unintuitive and unnecessarily difficult to land blows on the flying adversaries. Sometimes, your fellow workers succeed immediately, and at other times, they miss perpetually until they are slain. It is baffling. Sometimes, too, I wanted my comrades to remain somewhere only for them to throw themselves onto the enemy shortly thereafter. It is issues like these that make this otherwise fun game frustrating at times.

Overall, “Tonight We Riot” is an excellent and fun game whose efforts are undermined only by its occasionally cumbersome controls. It is certainly worth playing.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">72</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>b9372b72-4229-11eb-bd9a-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>A Short Game Burdened by Its Controls</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">96</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9bb60aa-f14b-11eb-aa57-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9bb60aa-f14b-11eb-aa57-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-07-30T18:35:10</review:date>
        <review:description>Immediately, props for possibly the coolest configuration menu in any game. Making the configuration menu part of the game&apos;s story is something I don&apos;t think I&apos;ve ever seen. This feels like it&apos;s trying to take notes off of the DOOM reboot, but it really runs home with what that game was trying to do. Put simply, DOOM 2016 is horse shit compared to this. When you melee an enemy for health, it doesn&apos;t lock you in place and make you immune to damage for a few seconds so you can just spam F and get free health along with some breathing room. In this game, it&apos;s a simple transaction. Hit enemy with your fist, get health. So there&apos;s a lot more strategy involved with that. The rocket launcher/shotgun hybrid is a really cool idea. 

Really unexpected twist towards the end of the demo, too. This demo has tons of potential.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>b9bb60aa-f14b-11eb-aa57-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Dopest shit I&apos;ve played in a decade</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9be16ac-b28c-11e8-9d05-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9be16ac-b28c-11e8-9d05-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2017-12-09T20:24:08</review:date>
        <review:description>Aside from the many criticisms other people have already left about the gameplay and AI, this game has probably the most disappointing ending you could imagine. I won&apos;t spoil it here, but I&apos;d urge you to look up the ending on youtube rather than buying the game if you really want to know how it ends.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">12</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>b9be16ac-b28c-11e8-9d05-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Don&apos;t get suckered in by the story</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">92</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9d69ece-296e-11eb-bfe0-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/b9d69ece-296e-11eb-bfe0-fa163e9aa6fa">
