        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c262ceca-948b-11e8-94fa-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c262ceca-948b-11e8-94fa-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2011-11-21T05:01:49</review:date>
        <review:description>I honestly don&apos;t understand what everyone sees in this game. I played this game the day after it was originally released. I wasn&apos;t impressed with it then and I&apos;m not impressed with it now. Aside from the clever humor in the game it&apos;s actually pretty dull, right down the to art style. Everything in the game is butt ugly and a horrible mix of brown and/or black. The mechanics and basic gameplay are varied and interesting at first. But after a few levels, I just couldn&apos;t even force myself to play this anymore. I can only imagine that, the people who are praising this, are only enjoying it for it&apos;s ugly, yet unique, visual esthetic and sense of humor. That being said, I can&apos;t recall a time when the humor or visual esthetic of a game made it more fun to play and this one is no different.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c262ceca-948b-11e8-94fa-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>What are they seeing?</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c2767fd6-fff7-11ea-9904-fa163edf6d96 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c2767fd6-fff7-11ea-9904-fa163edf6d96">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-09-26T15:56:57</review:date>
        <review:description>This is a linear single player game. It is made for a controller/gamepad in mind, but I did finish it on Keyboard + Mouse combination.

Technically, yes there are frame-drops here and there, yet, I did manage, to play the game without a single crash.

Played on: AMD Ryzen 1600, 16 GB RAM, Radeon RX570 8 GB, Windows 10 Home, SATA SSD. 

The animation and faces looks sometimes funny, like a mix of serious semi-real faces and than there are some faces/characters, that for whatever reasons reminds me of these anime characters from games like Resident Evil and such.

The environment graphics, textures, shadows, flames etc. ) look very good. They did even added some generic people, that walk around, so that you don&apos;t have the feel, that the world is very empty. It is using the Unreal Engine. 

I also like the sound effects. They are really spooky sometimes.
The story is nothing special, but ok.

I did finish the game in about 8 hours, because I died many times in one segment, where you need to go to a tower and there is a lift with enemies in three segments. Well, the last (top) segment is brutal. After using the green power (that Belial gave as a gift), that has the option, to heel yourself, I finally manage to go further and finish it.

The ending was in a cliffhanger, so maybe, we get a second part. At least I hope so.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c2767fd6-fff7-11ea-9904-fa163edf6d96</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>It has problems, but very playable</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">15</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c289c7a2-93fd-11e8-9127-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c289c7a2-93fd-11e8-9127-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2009-05-24T14:47:39</review:date>
        <review:description>One word describes this game experience. I first played this game on a Pentuim 233 with a 3dfx Voodoo3 card in 16-bit color, and then an early ATI Radeon in 32-bit, and it was still playable when the local area was full of fighters and capital ships. I had cut my space sim teeth on another very capable movie-based title, but this was the first time I felt myself becoming part of a futuristic universe. The ability to map any axis or button to any control, and the general (great) feel of piloting the different fighter craft all become unimportant and secondary to the plot, brought about by great voice acting and believable mission progression.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c289c7a2-93fd-11e8-9127-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Fun</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c2955a40-94d9-11e8-8ec6-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c2955a40-94d9-11e8-8ec6-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2012-12-25T01:39:22</review:date>
        <review:description>I played this game when I think i was 12/13 and probably the first game to have such strongly adult-oriented violence/cursing. It definitely marked my game memories. 
I remember playing this and fighting hand to hand at first and beating up bums to get a measly dollar here and there to afford a pistol or a lead pipe so i could bash in the local wannabe thugs. 
There were functions for speaking out loud to intimidate and harass. Though being on the Quake2 engine, it still had a different feel. the environment, controls, violence... everything is just so dark and the cypress hill soundtrack really made me feel gangster lol 

This game is something worth checking out and playing for sure. I wish this was remade (i know you are thinking GTA or something, but I mean a real gritty and dark game of thug violence haha )</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c2955a40-94d9-11e8-8ec6-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Gratuitous </review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


