    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c33a722e-2f27-11eb-bd54-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c33a722e-2f27-11eb-bd54-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-11-25T16:08:59</review:date>
        <review:description>Schön, dass hier dieses Spiel auftaucht. 
Es hatte ja damals nicht den großen kommerziellen Erfolg, obwohl es soviel eigentlich nicht falsch macht und ja auch nach Release noch gut gepatcht wurde. 
Man muss halt die Zeit haben, Texte zu lesen und sollte sich auf ein eher gemütliches Spielen einstellen. Dafür wird man in eine tiefe Story wie in ein Buch hineingezogen.
DSA Fans sollten sich eigentlich wohlfühlen, zumal es so viele Spiele in der DSA Welt ja gar nicht gibt.

Ich finde das Spiel auch heute noch grafisch hübsch anzuschauen und für den kleinen Preis im momentanen Sale (2,59 Euro) bekommt man sehr viel Spielzeit, die je nach Spielstil bei 80 Stunden liegen sollte. So lange hatte ich damals glaube ca. in den ersten Durchgang gesteckt.

Man hat eine schöne Auswahl aus vielen Anfangscharakteren und lernt viele Figuren noch im Spiel selbst kennen, die einen dann begleiten.

Im Grunde kann man viele Gruppenkonstellationen austesten und je nachdem, wie man unterwegs ist, unterscheiden sich auch Gespräche voneinander.

Die Story will ich nicht spoilern, aber wer das Spiel noch gar nicht kennt, bekommt sehr viele verschiedene Gegenden geboten und eine Story, die zum Ende hin sich immer mehr steigert....wenn man Zeit hat..^^

Von mir gibt es die 5 Sterne und ich kann nun endlich die alte CD entsorgen :-)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c33a722e-2f27-11eb-bd54-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Klare Empfehlung für Storyliebhaber</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">57</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c34238d0-d38e-11ec-8b71-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c34238d0-d38e-11ec-8b71-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-05-14T17:04:25</review:date>
        <review:description>NOT RECOMMENDED. You are supposed to escort the suicidal followers to the finish line in order to keep getting upgrades that will make the later missions slightly less like torment. IT is not easy, more like the extreme opposite of fun. I have no idea who this game is for. The level 4-4 is bad enough alone to lower the rating of the game down to 2/5 - you are practically at the mercy of pure RNG there. The stupidest part is how the ranged shooters cause more damage and take vastly more beating to silence, than the armored riot policemen. I do not have the patience to itemize the flaws further - it is that much pure suffering to actually play and is very frustrating (i.e. do not play this if you have depressive issues, your issues are likely to worsen). I noticed slight performance issues with the level ending and starting transitions, nothing game-breaking.

Regarding to the 2-player support: yeah, multiplayer is always better in any game. This not a game I would subject my contacts to and expect the relation not to worsen. You are better off with a better game for it such as Door Kickers and even Expendable. I have seen the end and know there is no point in giving this game a chance - there is nothing at the end of the rainbow except a survival mode with more idiot escort suffering. Speaking of Suffering, it is another game more worthy of your hard-earner mula.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c34238d0-d38e-11ec-8b71-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Awful Escort Missions</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c35181b4-9553-11e8-a38a-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c35181b4-9553-11e8-a38a-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2014-09-06T03:55:33</review:date>
        <review:description>Younger I was really fond of space shooter, and I played every one I could find: X-Wing series, Wing Commander series, I-war, etc.

Among all these games, Freespace 2 shines in my memory and took the first place of my personal ranking. The excellent graphics, the dramatic story, the ship design, the weapons sound, the various missions: everything contributes to build a fantastic atmosphere in this game.

If you like spacer shooters, you cannot miss Freespace 1 &amp;amp; 2. By the way it would be better to play the first before the second, to avoid Freespace 1 story spoil. But I fear FS1 won&apos;t have as well as FS2...</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c35181b4-9553-11e8-a38a-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Best space shooter ever played</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c3599c84-93fd-11e8-bbde-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c3599c84-93fd-11e8-bbde-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2009-05-25T00:42:04</review:date>
        <review:description>I played a demo of this when I was ten years old, and I remember thinking it was great.  When I saw it on GoG, I bought it right away, thinking five dollars would at least be worth the nostalgia it would provide.  Turns out the game doesn&apos;t really change after the first half-hour on display in the demo.

I probably could&apos;ve lived with the dated control scheme.  Even the rather mindless combat (hold down shoot and zigzag) and repetitive gameplay would&apos;ve been tolerable.  But in a game with levels that last from half an hour to an hour, a complete lack of checkpoints or any kind of intra-level save system is tantamount to a mortal sin.  All these factors combine to make MDK an exercise in frustration.

The game&apos;s aesthetics are certainly top-notch, and it&apos;s sense of humour is a nice addition, though it&apos;s never laugh-out-loud funny.  But all that can be gleaned from watching someone else suffer through the game on Youtube.  I&apos;m told it&apos;s short, only six levels long.  I only made it through four before kicking it off my hard drive.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c3599c84-93fd-11e8-bbde-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Could</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">11</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c37bed00-94b3-11e8-8c93-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c37bed00-94b3-11e8-8c93-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2012-04-22T18:11:14</review:date>
        <review:description>Freespace the space-sim always a game that you could be there and spend a little while with. 
Most of my play time with this game when I have been has been on bursts of a few missions till i got bored and did something else. 

The reason why ?: You needed to break it up. This game has no replay value until you fully forget it. 

Graphics: very good for its time. 

Controls/Gameplay: 
All over the keyboard and the joystick. slowing down the game play. But this predates alot of the more advanced mouse tech. That being said an disable mouse invert should have been included as it was with the joystick. 

This program really did need the various interminable training missions for you to keep up and also its reference card. Because you will routinely forget what you just learned.

Story: Very easy to follow but the cinematics are more or less filler and don&apos;t seem to be necessary at all as the information and briefings do cover what you need to know.

Overall 3/5. 
Conclusion: For a space-sim the easy to follow story and great graphics are usually a must as the emphasis on combat is very high. 
However when it comes to playing that combat its what makes the game take a downfall as it should have had easier controls to work with. 
With no replay value to the game I will need to wait till I forget to play again.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>c37bed00-94b3-11e8-8c93-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Freespace Review</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/c3830ab6-c8a5-11ec-8efe-fa163e99870e -->

