    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d00ed8d2-2f4f-11eb-93b7-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d00ed8d2-2f4f-11eb-93b7-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-11-25T20:55:40</review:date>
        <review:description>I played the pen &amp;amp; paper it was based on and the DVD version when it first came out. Loved every second of it. It recreates the world in one of my favourite historic phases (there is thousands of years of lore available in German), makes the rule system a little easier to handle (there where earlier games which adapted it almost 100%, which to non-P&amp;amp;P-Player could have been quite overwhelming, I suspect), and the I really liked the mix of epic storyline and oftentimes quite mundane sidequests. The latter point was one I liked most about The Black Eye: Not every adventure had you out to save the world, on the contrary, especially on lower levels you had everything from local village quarrels to kingdom-spanning conspiracies.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>d00ed8d2-2f4f-11eb-93b7-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Great adaptation of one of my favourite fantasy worlds</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">24</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d028c874-94dc-11e8-b611-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d028c874-94dc-11e8-b611-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2013-01-15T06:22:14</review:date>
        <review:description>...and thankfully GOG made that clear, but you know the story right?

When it came out the game was clearly called &apos;Descent: Freespace&apos; (and Conflict: Freespace in some places) so when you see it in a store you&apos;re probably thinking &apos;Oh boy! a new Descent game!&apos; and in my personal experience, if it had an ad for Descent 3 on the back of the box then that would of made it even more confusing in the end.

What this little confusion has done is expose many to one of the most innovative space fighters of all time, so all was not lost and particularly not on me.

If you like Descent, then go play it! all the games are available here already! But if you like high speed dog fighting action, exciting anti-capital ship operations and more, in space, don&apos;t jump out on this one!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>d028c874-94dc-11e8-b611-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Spoiler Alert: This is not Descent</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


