    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2cad45c-9566-11e8-a5db-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2cad45c-9566-11e8-a5db-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2014-11-23T02:49:08</review:date>
        <review:description>Yes, this game has problems. It&apos;s true that the second half was rushed and that the first city you see is by far the best. But. It&apos;s a very original game and has some unique merits. I mean, I&apos;ve played it about a decade ago yet still remember it vividly. I use it as the golden standard when describing a properly written evil path. In every single game, being evil means being a school-yard-bully type of douche bag. At best, it means joining the antagonist. This game instead does it right. You get to be a proper conniving and calculated irredeemable spawn of Satan. So, if you care to see how gloriously evil characters can be written, play this game and be evil. But know that this requires 180 point in speech, if I remember right, and that joining the Mages and then the Necromancers is probably the best route to darkness.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>d2cad45c-9566-11e8-a5db-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>The best implemented evil path, ever.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2e4121a-cc67-11eb-a55a-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2e4121a-cc67-11eb-a55a-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-06-13T19:53:05</review:date>
        <review:description>Thought I would liked this game being its a Fire Emblem, turn based strategy.  The story really is quite poor and there are no voice overs you have to read all the lines.  While reading stuff isn&apos;t horrible, it&apos;s the way they present the story, it doesn&apos;t make sense and lacks any true draw to care about the characters or world.  The game itself doesn&apos;t seem to have any major issues or bugs that I could see.  While I am not a big graphics person, the unit icons made it difficult to tell what you were about to fight.  Some of the mechanics I didn&apos;t actually see working like combing units in attack I never saw the other units attack they just stood there while the attacker did their thing.  Also the skill tree really needs a lot of work its very small and didn&apos;t have any intresting skills.  Lastly I didn&apos;t see a way of advancing your characters other then adding gems that added to some attributes.  All in all I don&apos;t even recommend this game for turn based junkies like me, its boring and the story diffently doesn&apos;t draw you in and make you want to play the game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>d2e4121a-cc67-11eb-a55a-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Sub par story and game play</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">14</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2f292ea-c693-11e8-ba2a-00163e4e60cc -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2f292ea-c693-11e8-ba2a-00163e4e60cc">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-10-03T01:38:03</review:date>
        <review:description>THE GAME - 5 stars

The game has its flaws - clunky controls, stiff animations, bad collisions. However, it has wonderful level design, which gets better and better, in the tradition of the early Tomb Raider games. There are also very interesting locations and story-driven interactions that surpass the early Tomb Raider games. 

If you are not bothered by the flaws and like to explore intricate, interconnected levels, vast locations that have no hand-holding (unless you activate hints in the map) and solve environmental puzzles, this is the game for you.

GOG VERSION - 2 STARS

- The game supports modern resolutions - that&apos;s really nice. 

- Runs immediately on modern machines without hassle - also nice.

- Unfortunately, decades ago, LucasArts did introduce a showstopper bug in version 1.2 of a re-release of the game. Because of it, in level 7, the Palawan temple, the game gets stuck on a cut-scene and it&apos;s impossible to proceed.

The GoG version currently (October 2018) has exactly the same bug - completely unchanged, because it&apos;s based on the same officially released, but broken, 1.2 version.
You&apos;ll still need to look for fan made patches that fix this bug online. It&apos;s not easy to find these (there are some fake ones). There was a German site that had all fixes. Good luck searching for them.

GOG GUIDELINES CLARIFICATION

GoG guidelines say I should not discuss technical issues in the review, but this is not a technical issue that the Support team can help with. It&apos;s a decade old showstopper bug, introduced by an official v1.2 patched CD re-release.

As a result, currently (October 2018) YOU WILL NOT BE ABLE TO GO BEYOND LEVEL 7 unless you look for a fan-made patch. 

I won&apos;t ask for a refund because GoG put in the effort to get the game in the store, and just this effort deserves support, but unless their QA start making full playthrough tests, or look online for fanmade patches BEFORE releasing on GoG, they&apos;ll keep offering half-functioning versions like this. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">44</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>d2f292ea-c693-11e8-ba2a-00163e4e60cc</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Exploration, puzzles, and version 1.2</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">354</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2fe5bb8-94a5-11e8-867b-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2fe5bb8-94a5-11e8-867b-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2012-02-28T19:18:40</review:date>
        <review:description>my favorite space flight sim of all time.  Better than the Wing Commanders, maybe even better than TIE Fighter!  I always described this to my friends as &quot;the best Star Wars game there is&quot;, since its battles feel more like the huge fleet battles from SW than the actual SW games ever did to me.
Cannot recommend this one highly enough!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>d2fe5bb8-94a5-11e8-867b-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Fantastic</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2fff130-b2c3-11e8-8165-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/d2fff130-b2c3-11e8-8165-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-04-29T17:08:28</review:date>
        <review:description>	The main menu of Virginia says “Press Enter to Take a Trip”. That is about as perfect a way to describe the game as I can: “A Trip”. This is like if David Lynch made a game. The whole thing feels like an acid trip of sorts. There is no dialogue at all so the game depends on facial reactions; music; and visual clues to make sure you can figure out what is going on. Luckily for Virginia those three things were the game’s strong points. The music was especially fantastic throughout the game. The graphics stunning but had a very simplistic quality that managed to be more detailed than I expected in it’s own way. I won’t pretend I understood the whole game but I understood enough to appreciate the story and the ability of the developers to intertwine various stories to all flow together without using voice acting. Gameplay wise it is mostly a walking sim as you are finding  what objects to interact with.

	I played Virginia on Linux. It never crashed on me and I didn’t notice any glitches. The game only has options for resolution; motion blur and a toggle for AA. There is a frame limiter but it only had options for 30 and 60 FPS. I would have preferred a Vsync option. I left it at unlimited rather than 30 or 60. The game has an auto save system but it never tells you exactly when it’s saving so the one time I exited the game and reloaded my game I was further back then expected. I would have preferred a manual save system. Alt-Tab Works.

Disk Space Used: 4.04 GB
Input Used: Keyboard + Mouse

Details Used: 1080P; AA on; Motion Blur Off
GPU Usage: 77-100 %
VRAM Usage: 1498-2053 MB
CPU Usage: 35-60 %
RAM Usage: 3.1-3.6 GB
Frame Rate: 79-228 FPS</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>d2fff130-b2c3-11e8-8165-00163e97122a</review:id>
