    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dac14f9a-9400-11e8-ae1a-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dac14f9a-9400-11e8-ae1a-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2011-03-20T13:27:20</review:date>
        <review:description>I joined GOG because of Freespace 2. The game was brilliantly constructed, I felt so engaged by the story and gameplay. You never learn your character&apos;s name, you&apos;re just a no-name pilot who&apos;s skills earn him (or her) spots in elite fighter squadrons. The game makes you feel small, whether it&apos;s by flying past a capital ship, or by being in another star system while a major space battle occurs else where. However, even though you feel small, you aren&apos;t insignificant. Every enemy bomber you shoot down saves lives aboard allied ship, and every capital ship you wreck protects the special equipment and crews aboard allied freighters. 

If you are a fan of military science fiction, especially the Babylon 5 TV show and the Honor Harrington book series, do yourself a favor and pick up this fantastic game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>dac14f9a-9400-11e8-ae1a-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Great Science Fiction</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


