    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dc8b0040-b21a-11e8-a28b-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dc8b0040-b21a-11e8-a28b-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2017-03-17T13:36:54</review:date>
        <review:description>This game was and still is fantastic!

To get around some of the issues caused by its age (640x480 resolution for example), get also FS2 on gog, then get the Freespace Open installer and get the pack that integrates FS1 into FS Open. You&apos;ll need the gog version of these games for the assets.

You&apos;ll find it to look and run amazing on modern hardware. i think even VR support is either already in, or not far anymore.

Putting Freespace into open source was a great great thing the Volition founders did back then!!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>dc8b0040-b21a-11e8-a28b-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Great game, use MODs!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">14</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dca68322-b299-11e8-840c-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dca68322-b299-11e8-840c-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-01-06T06:14:09</review:date>
        <review:description>Atlantic Fleet has many positives: It is turned based, which is always fun. There seems to be a wide variety of units and weapon options to choose from. The developers have made the effort to include stand-alone battles and campaigns. The models, textures and  water/lighting effects are very nice and compare favourably to more expensive games.

However, the negatives come close to neutralising the good. THERE IS NO SAVE OPTION. You can&apos;t get back to the main menu without throwing the battle and throwing away your hard earned fleet, which is a hideous waste and leads to grinding. Grinding is the worst aspect of gaming and a waste of precious time. You earn Renown (the currency for buying your ships) slowly and may need to grind the same scenario repeatedly, but success seems to reduce the pay-out for replayed battles. You cannot name your own ships.

The U-Boats have too much of an advantage (historically accurate, perhaps) which isn&apos;t fun. Your first encounter with U Boats can wipe out your fleet, meaning you may have to grind your way back to that scenario again from the beginning.

I lost my precious, hard earned ships twice - once simply trying to get to the menu to restart a battle (with the forced scuttle mechanic) and again with the first U Boat battle (I sunk one of the two subs to save some pride). However any game which forces me to restart twice is highly likely to see me write it off as unenjoyable and either not play it again or uninstall it.

I would prefer the developers to think about what turned based gamers really enjoy... Customisation of units, names, and load and save options...

If you think you would be frustrated by having ships you were becoming attached to destroyed or lost due to the one-shot all or nothing aspect of gameplay, I suppose you might give Atlantic Fleet a miss.

However, with a few fairly small changes it is a 4/5 title for me.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">20</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>dca68322-b299-11e8-840c-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Great potential but missing key features</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">157</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dcc18a5c-93fc-11e8-985c-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dcc18a5c-93fc-11e8-985c-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2009-04-24T08:58:35</review:date>
        <review:description>6 massive levels of CARNAGE!

MDK has a lot of charm for such a violent game, because there&apos;s just tons of zaniness going like. From the Dog Bone Airstrikes to the World Smallest Nuclear Explosion, there&apos;s surely something here to make you chuckle.

MDK does what others games usually do not, it gets the balance of action, platforming and puzzle work just right. As when you&apos;re not fighting for your life, you&apos;re trying to not fall off something or trying to figure out just what the hell to do next.

It&apos;s fantasticly simple and fiendishly addictive, the only downside to MDK, is that it ends. 6 levels just simply isn&apos;t enough. And I&apos;m not saying that because I don&apos;t think the game is value for money, because it truly is, I&apos;m saying it because if this game hooks you like it hooked so many gamers back in the day, you&apos;ll want more :)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>dcc18a5c-93fc-11e8-985c-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Brilliant!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dccbf0e4-bd55-11ea-9e21-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dccbf0e4-bd55-11ea-9e21-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-07-03T20:51:46</review:date>
        <review:description>Shantae And The Seven Sirens brings the series back to its metroidvania roots, yet maintains the gorgeous hand drawn visuals and animations of Half-Genie Hero. This time with fully animated cutscenes by Studio Trigger! 

There&apos;s not much to complain about other than Jake Kaufman didn&apos;t work on the soundtrack, so his themes are absent, the game is WAY too easy, and finding all of the hidden collectibles is a SLOG. Other than that, the game is a lot of fun, and still contains all the charm, excitement, and waifus of a Shantae game, and introduces new mechanics that enhance the experience. 

Full review:
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FZRwWhqBxKA</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>dccbf0e4-bd55-11ea-9e21-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Waifuvania Return To Form!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dcd25ce4-b2d6-11e8-90b8-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dcd25ce4-b2d6-11e8-90b8-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-06-08T20:43:53</review:date>
        <review:description>Wenn ein Spiel versucht die besten Zutaten bereits bestehender Game-Franchises herauszupicken, um diese miteinander zu verschmelzen, kann man wohl einen echten Top-Titel erwarten.
Leider ist Lionheart schon von Beginn an alles andere als herausragend.
Ist jedoch nur halb so schlimm, denn trotz der ganzen Macken war der Titel doch recht spielenswert ... Allerdings nur in der ersten Spielhälfte!
Ab einem gewissen Spielzeitpunkt, zeigt uns „Legacy of the Crusader“ nämlich sein zweites, hässliches Gesicht: Ein mittelmäßiges Hack&apos;n&apos;Slay-RPG mit eklatanten Fehlern, welche in dieser Form einfach inakzeptabel sind.
Mit diesem Spiel hätte man der Iso-RPG-Ära zu einem schönen Abschluss verhelfen können.
Dummerweise legten sich die Leute von der Schwarzen Insel auf die faule Haut, während Reflexive nur die Rohfassung eines guten Spieles zusammenprogrammierten.
Ab und zu mal den „Anderen“ über die Schulter zu schauen reicht eben nicht aus, Black Isle.
Ausgiebiges Probespielen mit anschließendem Feinschliff wäre hier absolut notwendig gewesen - leider hat man diese zusätzliche Arbeit scheinbar gescheut.
Was bleibt ist ein interessantes Spiel, welches anfangs durchaus unterhaltsam ist, aber letztendlich nur langweilt und dafür sorgt, dass die alten Baldurs Gate-, Fallout- oder Diablo-Disks aus dem Schrank hervorgekramt werden ...

Pro und Kontra:

Pro:
- Charaktergenerierung nach SPECIAL-System
- originelles Szenario
- variable Quests zu Beginn des Spieles

Kontra:
- sehr langweiliges und träges Kampfsystem
- wird ab einen gewissen Zeitpunkt viel zu kampflastig
- langweilige 08/15 Story
- zahlreiche Detailmacken</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>dcd25ce4-b2d6-11e8-90b8-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Halbgarer CRPG-Mischmasch</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dcf39f3a-f776-11ea-91c4-fa163edf6d96 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dcf39f3a-f776-11ea-91c4-fa163edf6d96">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-09-15T20:14:07</review:date>
        <review:description>Ich habe das Original nie gespielt. Als ich von der Remastered-Version gelesen habe, habe ich immer gehofft, dass es auch auf GOG erscheint und hier ist es nun. Wochenlang habe ich mich gefreut, viel gelesen, hier und da zwei, drei Folgen eines Lets Plays auf YT geschaut und nun kann ich es selbst spielen. Mal von den &quot;kleinen&quot; Bugs abgesehen, habe ich genau das bekommen, was ich erwartet habe. Eine MENGE Spass, RP und sehr viel Action. Ich liebe diese Welt, ich liebe die Charaktere, ich mag all die kleinen Erzaehlungen der Charaktere. Man glaubt es gar nicht, wie viel sie zu erzaehlen haben. Selbst, wenn man sie nach Tagen mal wieder besucht, gibt es hier und da sehr oft auch neues, was sie zu erzaehlen haben. Und wenn sie sich nur dazu aeussern, wie der eigene Charakter gehandelt hat in der ein oder anderen Quest. 

Ich mag die Grafik und ich mag auch das Kampfsystem oder die Talente. Ehrlich gesagt, ich liebe dieses ganze Spiel, auch wenn es nach hinten hinaus, etwas zu leicht wird. Der Charakter wird wirklich maechtig. Ich meine so richtig Maechtig :) Aber vielleicht sollte ich auch nur den Schwierigkeitsgrad weiter anheben? ;)

&quot;Kingdoms of Amalur: Re-Reckoning&quot; ist genau DAS Spiel, was ich derzeit gebraucht habe. Lieben Dank! &amp;lt;3</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>dcf39f3a-f776-11ea-91c4-fa163edf6d96</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Mein Rendezvous mit KoA: Re-Reckoning &amp;lt;3</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dd28097c-9441-11e8-9277-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/dd28097c-9441-11e8-9277-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2010-04-29T21:35:36</review:date>
        <review:description>i grew up on this game and frankly i cried the day my disks stopped working the only other copy was a collectors box on ebay for like 300 bucks i am glad to see someone is snapping up these old clasics and redistributing them for the next generation. but i have freespace to thank for my piloting skills. recently i started playing a game called eternal silence and frankly freespace is what gave me the ability to have a 10 - 1 kill ratio in space sims. sadly the other players realised that i suck at fps and now keep sniping be before i can get into the interceptors XD. but freespace is was an will always be great game</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
