    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ebea1d9a-58a9-11eb-9dcd-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ebea1d9a-58a9-11eb-9dcd-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-01-17T11:53:59</review:date>
        <review:description>Having played (and loved) Lucas Pope&apos;s &quot;Papers, please&quot; previously, I knew that this game would be worth buying and playing. However, I decided time and time again not to make the purchase, mostly because of the 2-color graphics. 

Now that I&apos;ve finally bought and completed the game, I have to say that the artistic style only helps to make the story that more mysterious.

The game doesn&apos;t hold your hand, so determining &quot;the fates&quot; can be hard at times. Only after playing over half of the game I noticed things, that could have helped me really determine a person&apos;s identity, instead of guessing it. That doesn&apos;t determine from giving the game a five star rating.

It&apos;s been a long time since I&apos;ve been this captivated by a story in a game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>ebea1d9a-58a9-11eb-9dcd-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A captivating mystery</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


