    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ed669294-b201-11e8-a7dd-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/ed669294-b201-11e8-a7dd-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2016-12-26T06:19:43</review:date>
        <review:description>Here is a game that, after all these years since it came out, still makes me play it! It&apos;s awesome, the voices, the graphics, the gameplay. Enemies shooting you left and right. Your squadron buddies asking for your help, the heat of the battle! The incredible momentum of a true dog fight in space. 
Sometimes you can almost feel it&apos;s real (your heart races so much you almost end up with a heart attack)! One of the best games (in it&apos;s category) ever made for sure. 
Of course the ships aren&apos;t detailed like the ones we see today in more modern games. But that&apos;s part of it&apos;s charm. I think. This game had a lot of known voices of Hollywood: Robert Loggia (Independence Day), Ronny Cox (Total Recall), Kurtwood Smith (Robocop), Stephen Baldwin (Backdaft, The hunt for the red october)... Just to mention a few... About making a movie out of Freespace, well, why not, even if it&apos;s only to see how this story ends... At the very least, a Freespace3 Game. I&apos;d Buy it even for as much as €50!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>ed669294-b201-11e8-a7dd-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Freespace 2 Rocks!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</review:upvotes>
