    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0f938f40-f4db-11e8-8986-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0f938f40-f4db-11e8-8986-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-11-30T22:03:53</review:date>
        <review:description>Le jeu est fantastique, bien q&apos;un peu rebuter au premier abord par son design, il est très immersif et l&apos;histoire est passionante. Nous avons joué à deux avec mon mari et nous avions beaucoup de mal à nous arrêter tant le jeu est prenant. Le gameplay est simple mais efficace ce qui permet une totale immersion dans l&apos;histoire. Un des meilleurs jeu que j&apos;ai joué dernièrement. 

One of the best game I play this year. It&apos;s very immersive and the history is really interresting ! We play together (my husband and I) and we had passed some evenings on it. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>0f938f40-f4db-11e8-8986-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Un merveilleux cluedo / Amazing Cluedo</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0f9d8780-b21d-11e8-96f4-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0f9d8780-b21d-11e8-96f4-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2017-03-24T02:43:52</review:date>
        <review:description>In today&apos;s world of Armas and Squads/Insurgencies, to put Delta Force as a military simulator is kind of like letting a special needs kid join the school football team; it&apos;s easy to forget that back then Half-Life had just been released and Doom clones were still a thing. As such, to have an open world map was more than enough to give you a stiffy. Having friendly AI and choppers was an extra bonus.

I loved this game when I was a kid but it&apos;s hard to recommend it if you weren&apos;t into it at the right time. The AI was dumb but challenging, since once it would figure out where you were it could kill you in 2-3 shots (again, &quot;simulator&quot;), the gameplay revolved mostly about snipping black moving dots from very far away and watching their dot buddies crouch and go into &quot;alert&quot; mode (and do f*ck-all) or run-around brain dead attempting to find you. Then after spending 10 minutes just camping outside the enemy base slowly making your way in, checking the buildings corners and (usually) blowing up whatever objective you had on the menu in that particular mission.

In terms of gear you&apos;d get 5 weapons to choose from and 2 sideweapons. The UI... well... look at the screenshots... The map was a different button which you would have to check every 2 minutes to make sure an enemy wouldn&apos;t sneak up on you. Friendly AI was a bit moronic too yet used scarcely (max 4 on the same map except a few scenarios). It would sometimes die way before you even reached the main objective, however having a few survive and run alongside you to the extraction point was always a fun moment.

It was mindless entertainment but if you want a classic DF experience I&apos;d suggest starting straight with DF2 for the slightly better graphics/gameplay. Don&apos;t worry about the &quot;story&quot;. It&apos;s generic 90s military fiction i.e.: serviceable at best.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>0f9d8780-b21d-11e8-96f4-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Grand father of Military-Sims</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">27</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0f9fced4-60de-11ea-a683-fa163e93294d -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0f9fced4-60de-11ea-a683-fa163e93294d">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-03-08T03:42:24</review:date>
        <review:description>I think it is one of those games that try to be weird for the sake of being weird. And it tries too hard. There is no subtlety here. Weird stuff happens. Is it the plague? Are you hallucinating? Oh wait, you are somewhere else. You died. No wait, you are alive. Eventually, I just stopped trying to figure it out.

It is like the old PSX Lucid Dream Simulator, but they did not pretend to have any meaning and/or mystery behind it all. It was just an experience.

Still, based on the rating, it clearly appeals to some gamers so I am going to leave it at that.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">36</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>0f9fced4-60de-11ea-a683-fa163e93294d</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Not what I hoped it would be</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">9</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0fa1f138-9a9d-11eb-a57e-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0fa1f138-9a9d-11eb-a57e-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-04-11T11:08:12</review:date>
        <review:description>A very nice turn-based sci-fi RPG, slightly reminiscent of the original Fallout.

.) Great character creation and progression.
.) Varied approach to many situtations - try convince others through diplomacy, attack them, or just sneak by
.) Runs well, had no problems with bugs or crashes so far
.) Intruiguing, gritty game world with believable characters
.) Has a certain &quot;cyberpunk&quot;-like vibe to it, with a dark world, implants and augmentations, and some powerful factions wrestling for power and control, with little regards to the populace
.) the turn-based combat is great, and the skills and combat options you learn can pack a punch

Still being in development, this is not a finished game. There are a few areas that might benefit from future improvements:
.) Movement feels a bit clunky at times. It&apos;s not always clearly visible where you can walk and where you can&apos;t, and clicking somewhere you can&apos;t just makes your character stop dead in their tracks.
.) Balancing is not perfect yet - certain skills/items seem to be way more useful than others.
.) Difficulty is high (and spiky), and relies way too much on nonstop quicksaving and quickloading at every step you take - and there seem to be no difficulty settings to adjust.
</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">11</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>0fa1f138-9a9d-11eb-a57e-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A very nice turn-based sci-fi RPG</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">40</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0fa65e70-93fd-11e8-869b-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0fa65e70-93fd-11e8-869b-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
