    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0ff5d59a-f198-11ea-8f84-fa163e7ab9c9 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/0ff5d59a-f198-11ea-8f84-fa163e7ab9c9">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-09-08T08:56:39</review:date>
        <review:description>Nice sense of progression, gigantic map, tons of quests and equipment and dungeons to explore!
The graphics are charming and the combat is fast, fluid, and fair...and lethal. This is NOT a walk in the part power trip. Every scrap of armor and slightly sharper sword you covet. Each enemy you fell feels like an accomplishment. It&apos;s a bit like a less reflex based version of Gothic at best.
At worst I ran into a few quests that didn&apos;t register, it has crashed from startup a few times in windowed mode (but when it runs it hasn&apos;t crashed) and if you think you can grind levels by just killing random enemies you are in for a slog. This is the kind of RPG where finishing quests give you tons of loot and experience, but killing a dire wolf even if it leaves you half dead yields a handful of gold and a little blip of EXP. This does encourage you to look for rumors, quests, talk to people and poke around in caves and towers. 
So it&apos;s a rough diamond, but it&apos;s quite fun for what it is! If you like the World of Warcraft style loop, this is a fine lite example of that. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>0ff5d59a-f198-11ea-8f84-fa163e7ab9c9</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Tough Single Player MMO</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">15</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/00a50b76-2a9a-11ea-b0eb-00163e4e60cc -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/00a50b76-2a9a-11ea-b0eb-00163e4e60cc">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-12-30T02:19:11</review:date>
        <review:description>It&apos;s a shame it sold so badly, while run of the mill AAA trash rakes in billions. There&apos;s literally no other game like this one. It has the most interesting and eerie world I&apos;ve ever seen, the story is gripping and the clock ticking mercilessly takes the whole experience to another level. You can&apos;t save everyone, you can&apos;t do everything you want, but you&apos;ll sure as hell be trying to. Only here after tending to a patient you end up having to cross an infected district with no immunity boosters left, and then breaking into people&apos;s homes to steal food, because you are starving and you spent the last of your money on antibiotics.

Do yourself a favor. Play on the difficulty level labeled as &quot;intended&quot;. It&apos;s brutal, but that&apos;s an important part of the story and only makes the game better. After finishing it, I feel like playing on easy would impact the experience very negatively.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>00a50b76-2a9a-11ea-b0eb-00163e4e60cc</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Unique and atmospheric</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">11</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/00ad01c2-3bfb-11ec-84e4-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/00ad01c2-3bfb-11ec-84e4-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-11-02T18:36:17</review:date>
        <review:description>This game delivers what it promises. You build bridges using limited resources and test them under load.
Good points:
-Puzzles are rather challenging. Solving them is satisfying.
-Simulation &quot;Stress tests reveal&quot; really helpful and educational.
Bad points:
-Gameplay AKA &quot;bridge editor&quot; is not good. You cannot move linked point too far. Usually, only way to edit something is to delete parts and draw them again.
-You cannot change sound &amp;amp; music volume, and actually it is better to completely turn them off and listen to your own music.
-You cannot change language.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>00ad01c2-3bfb-11ec-84e4-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>OK game.</review:title>
