    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/059a39fa-5377-11ec-ab55-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/059a39fa-5377-11ec-ab55-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-12-02T15:52:00</review:date>
        <review:description>Among metroidvanias/soulsvanias, Momodora is not the most challenging, nor the longest, nor the most innovative one.

It stands out because it&apos;s very polished and it&apos;s full of details that show that a lot of care has been put in it. Momodora creates a very good atmosphere, with an underlying tone of nostalgia, suffering, melancholy and loss. Character designs are pretty and imaginative, gameplay is agile, animations are fluid, pixellated girls are pretty, comic reliefs aren&apos;t annoying and they even manage to make you smile, and music is excellent.

We&apos;re Kaho, a forest priestess, sent to the corrupt capital to stop the power tainting the land and its inhabitants. We follow a vow of silence, and we are equipped with a bow, and an enchanted maple leaf we wield as a sword.

The &quot;Reverie Under the Moonlight&quot; subtitle tries to hide that this should have been Momodora 4. Momodora 1 to 3 are simple games where the designer gained experience and polished his formula. Kaho is a character alluded to in previous game, which makes this game a prequel. &quot;Reverie Under the Moonlight&quot; sure looks like the Magnum Opus of its creator.

Mechanically, it&apos;s a light soulsvania with the same healing and saving mechanics (bonfires and estus flasks, with different names and shapes). It can become difficult in certain places, but overall is an accesible game. Gamers should pick hard difficulty from the start. The game takes a while to let you have some freedom to explore, and feels more or less lineal. There&apos;s a bad ending and a less bad ending, depending if you picked one specific item or not.

You&apos;ll love Momodora if you don&apos;t expect too much from it, or if you are able to appreciate the little details and care its creator put in it. It won&apos;t be your favorite game, but you won&apos;t forget about it easily.

Oh, and there&apos;s a giant undead witch boss whose boobs are also her hitboxes. Ten out of ten.
</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>059a39fa-5377-11ec-ab55-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Small indie gem.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


