    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a249678-9578-11e9-9e22-ce2bf10ddff3 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a249678-9578-11e9-9e22-ce2bf10ddff3">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-06-23T05:31:07</review:date>
        <review:description>Back flip on a motorcycle while shooting, knock an explosive tank through a net to hit your enemy on the head, shoot a bullet off a frying pan, and many other stylish ways to kill your enemies. This is a game with style that makes you feel like a mix of John Wick and a bit of Deadpool at the same time.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1a249678-9578-11e9-9e22-ce2bf10ddff3</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Amazing Game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a2c626e-43cc-11eb-9b08-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a2c626e-43cc-11eb-9b08-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-12-21T22:35:45</review:date>
        <review:description>Pathologic hooked me from the start. The storytelling, the world, the characters, the mystery - everything perfectly interwoven. Big props also go to the excellent way it made use of the economy throughout, as you rarely get a game that has you considering so thoughtfully what you need most and how far you&apos;re willing to sink to get it. In most roleplaying games, when a weak character offers you a reward for helping them, you automatically turn it down to score the good boy points. Not so here. Because what you&apos;re being given could seriously make the difference whether someone lives or dies that day. If you&apos;ve got food on you, you might make the risky trip to help a person that needs treatment. But if you&apos;re low? Well, those are hours you simply can&apos;t spare because you&apos;re in dire need of supplies. 

Really, what I want to say is that you shouldn&apos;t feel too intimidated by the description if you want to check this out. I despise time constraints in most games, but it&apos;s so integral to the flow of your experience in Pathologic that you won&apos;t ever wish you could play it without it. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1a2c626e-43cc-11eb-9b08-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>What a game to play during a pandemic...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a31bf12-42c7-11eb-9979-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a31bf12-42c7-11eb-9979-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-12-20T15:27:27</review:date>
        <review:description>I find it hilarious that the same people who are complaining about how buggy this game is, or it&apos;s cost, are also the ones saying it is like &apos;Skyrim&apos; - the game that to this day can&apos;t be played without a community debug mod and is released at 60 dollars for every device on earth. As always the overall rating for this game is lower than the verified owners rating, which continues to prove that GOG should only allow those who buy a game to review it, to avoid these idiots being so upset at not being able to afford something, like they are entitled to it by birth or something, that they take it to multiple platforms. 

To the game, this is an amazing title by all accounts, and altough not perfect, no game is, it is very close. All NPC&apos;s are voiced which even nowadays is a hit or miss, the combat mechanics are smooth and satisfying and altough you can button mash good things await those who memorise the combos. The graphics are stunning, despite a lacking draw distance, and clearly a lot of work was put in making the graphics updated. The story is ok but its in the side missions that this game really shines. The loot is quite excessive and you will have to make regular trips to sell it all and the enemies are varied and well planned.

This is an absolute must-buy, I really suggest you ignore the negative comments as they are made by sad people who feel entitled to things in life. I have played for about 100 hours and I haven&apos;t ecountered a gamebreaking bug yet, but my game has had a couple of audio bugs, nothing that a reload can&apos;t sort out.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">12</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1a31bf12-42c7-11eb-9979-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>What an amazing title</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">21</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a646b8e-94e0-11e8-9742-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a646b8e-94e0-11e8-9742-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2013-02-07T17:53:11</review:date>
        <review:description>The game itself deserves 5 stars for sure. The MAC port, however, has some flaws:

1. The game runs in bad screen aspect ration. I don&apos;t know how it works on a laptop, but I had to correct aspect ratio on my external display every time I launched the game. I found no setting to correct it the software way.
2. Some models, especially ones for big ships, get messed up. Some textures blink for no apparent reason. Happened ti me in about 7-10 missions in the game.
3. At some point the game stopped starting up. I had to re-download and re-install it. Luckily my player profile was unharmed.

Apart from that, the game is perfectly playable and You can enjoy it as much as You did in the 90s. ;)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1a646b8e-94e0-11e8-9742-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>MAC version review</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a92bb1a-ab26-11eb-9dc3-fa163e3a9b6e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1a92bb1a-ab26-11eb-9dc3-fa163e3a9b6e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-05-02T12:09:30</review:date>
        <review:description>Three stars for the actual game: Above average, simple, entertaining, and definitely original setting/central idea. A solid 3/5. Good for them.

Plus one star for worker-owned company. We need more of those if we want to once again have some fresh ideas on gameplay and scenarios, like we used to years ago. To get out of the loop of rehashing the same &quot;proven&quot; recipes for games. To avoid having corporate heads that know nothing about gaming, keep messing up the developer&apos;s vision. Worker-owned companies. Good for them.

Plus another star because it was a free giveaway. Good for us.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">34</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1a92bb1a-ab26-11eb-9dc3-fa163e3a9b6e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Some simple math (3+1+1=5)</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">21</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1aab365e-b35e-11ec-affd-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1aab365e-b35e-11ec-affd-fa163e9aa6fa">
