    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c0ab9b4-9c6d-11ec-8df0-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c0ab9b4-9c6d-11ec-8df0-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-03-05T12:14:57</review:date>
        <review:description>Graphiquement, Amalur est coloré avec des petits effets sympathiques surtout dans la première &quot;grande zone&quot; (la forêt).

La musique est pas mal du tout en particulier celle quand on explore cette même zone citée plus haut : elle contribue à donner une ambiance unique. 

L&apos;histoire bien clichée n&apos;en est pas moins intéressante : on la suit avec plaisir. Mais le gros point fort à mon sens c&apos;est le peuple des Faes : je les trouve original et fascinant. Les pierres des Ballades qui racontent des récits permettent d&apos;étoffer le background de ce peuple ainsi que du monde où l&apos;on évolue. 

La durée de vie est énorme ! Pour ma part, je l&apos;ai terminé en 83 heures en fouillant bien partout. Nous avons des choix à faire mais franchement, ils sont anecdotiques. J&apos;aurai bien aimé qu&apos;on puisse avoir un réel impact sur le monde et non pas avoir juste un dialogue qui change avec une faction qui devient hostile. Décevant. 

Malgré ses qualités, ce jeu souffre de bien des problèmes : 

- Caméra qui part en vrille dans les combats quand on veut s&apos;enfuir : c&apos;est injouable ;

- PNJ qui parle sans ouvrir la bouche ;

- Clignotements disgracieux (les yeux des gnomes notamment) ;

- Murs invisibles : on se cogne constamment contre eux ;

- Des bugs jamais corrigés (décors qui volent par exemple) ;

- Le système de furtivité pété : vous pouvez tuer un ennemi et son copain à côté s&apos;en fiche ;

- Pas de vie dans les villes : les PNJ marchent sans but (où sont les enfants ?) ;

- Système de création de personnage pas très poussé (la personnalisation est assez limitée).

Je tiens à saluer le travail réalisé sur les DLCS : ils sont tous géniaux ! Leurs histoires sont cool notamment celle des Dents de Naros. Pour Kel, c&apos;est sympa mais le pauvre me fait de la peine : on le voit que deux ou trois fois et c&apos;est tout. Mal exploité. Dommage. 

Je conseille à tous d&apos;au moins essayer &quot;Les Royaumes d&apos;Amalur&quot;. Il faut un certain temps pour s&apos;adapter mais après : c&apos;est que du bonheur. Vivement une suite. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1c0ab9b4-9c6d-11ec-8df0-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Un univers avec du potentiel</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c0c97f2-89e5-11eb-9657-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c0c97f2-89e5-11eb-9657-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-03-21T03:31:07</review:date>
        <review:description>This is a great game. It requires some patience and reflection, but the payback is just priceless. I also find it philosophically challenging: you can&apos;t avoid relating the shade&apos;s experience with the human experience. The shade just finds itself in a world it does not know, does not understand and where its goal in life is completely unfathomable. The only thing it knows is that it needs to wait for its time to pass by, finding gratification in meaningless things like decorating its home. As every human, the shade feels desperately lonely and purposeless. However, it still finds ways to live with its condition and look for adventure and hope.
If the player is particularly adventurous, the shade will find itself digging into its own loneliness and darkness, in a desperate attempt to try to overcome them. However, as perfectly stated in the game, &quot;each triumph may be a veiled defeat. And for each defeat, there is a hidden triumph, lingering in the dark&quot;.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1c0c97f2-89e5-11eb-9657-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Best game I ever played</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">23</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c1332d2-92a7-11ea-872c-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c1332d2-92a7-11ea-872c-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-05-10T13:15:01</review:date>
        <review:description>Tonight We Riot is a fun little game. It&apos;s explicitly political and not interested in being subtle about it.

+ Love the idea of controlling a movement and not a strong leader
+ Gameplay is easy to understand and a lot of fun. It&apos;s fast pace and full of energy.
+ Soundtrack is a banger
+ Humor is great
+ Game is developed by a worker owned co-op

- Weapon selection is clunky at times, especially late game when you unlocked all weapons
- It is very short and besides an endlessmode there is not that much of a replay value to the game

All in all i&apos;d give the game a solid 4/5 but since there are so many right wing trolls review bombing this game i give it 5 stars to bump up the rating ;)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">154</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1c1332d2-92a7-11ea-872c-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Short, Simple, Fun</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">128</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c1515a2-1178-11eb-a11e-fa163edf6d96 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c1515a2-1178-11eb-a11e-fa163edf6d96">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-10-18T22:28:32</review:date>
        <review:description>I wish more games were made with this much heart.

The level of detail and cleverness is astounding. There is nearly no animation and whenever there would be one, it cuts to &quot;black&quot;; and yet the sound effects, the characters and freeze-framed situations have more in them happening, make it more alive and leave more impact than many so-called AAA titles.
The music is amazing and actually works in tandem with what&apos;s happening, which made me feel exhilarated.

Each match of 3 correctly deduced fates brings so much satisfaction it feels like it shouldn&apos;t be possible.

Sadly, not much of a replay value, and requires too much attention to detail to not randomly guess at some points but actually deduce the facts. Still, it&apos;s one of the very few games that are worth way, way more than you pay even at the full price.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1c1515a2-1178-11eb-a11e-fa163edf6d96</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>At a loss for words</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c462690-e91e-11ec-b577-42010a340c26 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c462690-e91e-11ec-b577-42010a340c26">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-06-11T03:33:27</review:date>
        <review:description>Bullets Per Minute is a banger metal soundtrack in which you shoot, reload, and dash to the beat destroying the legions of Norse hell.

While the game is initially pretty easy once you get the hang of it, the difficulty rapidly increases as you unlock other characters and their variously challenging kits. I finally reached 100% on Steam recently by spending over 26 hours in two weeks on just the challenges for the final 2 characters (Odr and Sanngrior).

The item spread isn’t incredible, but it follows in the footsteps of amazing roguelikes like Enter the Gungeon and Risk of Rain by giving you the chance to find yourself an incredibly overpowered run with one or two lucky drops. 

I’m a bit of a nut for this game (I’ve bought the soundtrack, 100%ed it as mentioned, and have argued it’s better than Doom 2016 in some ways), it’s absolutely amazing when you hit the flow state because the core gameplay is just so satisfying. If you like roguelikes, are decent at FPS games, and don’t mind the visuals, it may well turn you into a bit of a nut too.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1c462690-e91e-11ec-b577-42010a340c26</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Absolutely addictive</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c52ece4-0cc6-11eb-8ce1-fa163edf6d96 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1c52ece4-0cc6-11eb-8ce1-fa163edf6d96">
