    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1d372da2-956d-11eb-8cfb-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1d372da2-956d-11eb-8cfb-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-04-04T20:42:23</review:date>
        <review:description>It&apos;s one of those &quot;walking simulator&quot; type things. I&apos;ve played some bad ones, I&apos;ve played some good ones. This one falls squarely in the &quot;bad&quot; category for one sole reason: There is no reason to actually walk around and look at things, so why did the devs choose this form?

At any given time, you can only do one thing. And the environments don&apos;t really have any details for you to learn anything from, so there is no reason to look at anything else than whatever object is the one you&apos;re supposed to press &quot;action&quot; on right now. There are no spoken words and precious little writing. 

In other games of this sort, you explore your surroundings and learn from environmental details. Book titles, framed pictures, documents, items on shelves, etc. Not so in this game. Everything is empty and there is always only one thing that the directors of the game want you to pay attention to. Sometimes the game even takes control directly and forces you to look at something specific. Frankly, it might as well cut the pretenses and do this all the time. In other words, it might as well be a short film. But then, of course, it would have to compete with real movies and it would fall short.

I&apos;m currently also playing What Remains of Edith Finch. That game is everything Virginia fails to be. Go play that instead.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1d372da2-956d-11eb-8cfb-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Why does this exist?</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1d5c3fde-94d0-11e8-95b6-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1d5c3fde-94d0-11e8-95b6-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2013-03-11T03:19:50</review:date>
        <review:description>I dont have time to review this game because frankly i dont feel it deserves any time to play.

I may not have loads of evidence to prove it as of writing, but the instant i opened up the .exe my speakers bursted, not my external stereo speakers, my laptop&apos;s internal speakers bursted, emitting and ear shatteringly loud screetch, and huge static.

A computer recovery and luck fixed the issue, and the only thing i can blame right now, is this game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1d5c3fde-94d0-11e8-95b6-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Breaks speakers, breaks ears</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1dcf0c3e-53a6-11eb-9aac-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1dcf0c3e-53a6-11eb-9aac-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-01-11T02:44:09</review:date>
        <review:description>Als Spiel für den Einstieg in Simulationsspiele für den Freizeit-Wintersport gut geeignet. 

In der aktuellen Demo fehlen weitere Möglichkeiten des Gebäudebaus oder Verwaltungssegmentes, als Beispiel Restaurant, Cafés, Wandertouren, Schneeballschlachtfelder, Iglubau, Snowboard- und Kinderrodelbahnen.

Ebenso wäre eine Art Ressourcenmanagement angebracht als Beispiel für die Verwaltung von Personal, Einnahmen durch Verkäufe, Einteilung in Aufgaben. 

Ebenso wäre eine Tourismus-Naturerhaltung-Abhängigkeit in Betracht zu ziehen. Als Beispiel je mehr Touristen und Bahnen, desto häufiger die Wahrscheinlichkeit von Lawinen. 

Allem in Allem eine einfache und nette Skipisten-Simulation, welche Potential für mehr hat. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1dcf0c3e-53a6-11eb-9aac-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Erster Eindruck von Snowtopia-Demo</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1dedb6b2-1918-11ea-92fb-00163e4e60cc -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1dedb6b2-1918-11ea-92fb-00163e4e60cc">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
