    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/151e4ba4-1ccb-11e9-8efd-00163e4e60cc -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/151e4ba4-1ccb-11e9-8efd-00163e4e60cc">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-01-20T17:50:16</review:date>
        <review:description>I&apos;ve bought this on early access on Steam. It was fun to play even then. Now it has more packed plot, and I can feel it&apos;s upgrading and evolving. It reminds me of Harvest Moon and Stardew Valley titles although it&apos;s in 3D, not focused on farming only and world looks much bigger there ready for explore - half of it is still a mystery to me.  If you like such themes then I would totally recomend it to you :)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>151e4ba4-1ccb-11e9-8efd-00163e4e60cc</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Worth playing</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">11</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/15376cc4-66f4-11e9-aa2a-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/15376cc4-66f4-11e9-aa2a-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-04-25T03:50:12</review:date>
        <review:description>This is my favorite kind of game: short, clever and packed with ideas. It&apos;s a wholly unique gameplay mechanism paired with a wholly unique rendering engine, all combined to make about 5-8 hours of absorbing game.

The basic idea is that a ship, long thought to be lost, shows up again in Falmouth harbor, and you need to figure out what happened to the 60 crew and passengers. To that end, you have a mysterious stopwatch that will allow you to see the moment of each crew members&apos; deaths in frozen time, while you can walk around the scene and examine things from various angles, look for clues, etc.

The game strikes a great balance with the difficulty: it doesn&apos;t hold your hand, and some of the deductions are pretty hard, but with perseverance essentially anyone should be able to complete the game. If you end up guessing a bit, you&apos;re in good company. Every answer can be figured out from the clues given, but a few of the clues are very obscure, so in some cases eliminating some of the possibilities and then making some educated guesses may be your best option.

Definitely a milestone in gaming, and really a game that is not to be missed.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>15376cc4-66f4-11e9-aa2a-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Tremendous fun for would-be detectives</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1548d574-c3f5-11e8-b931-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1548d574-c3f5-11e8-b931-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-09-12T02:52:47</review:date>
        <review:description>I can&apos;t say that Visual Novels are my cup of tea in general. I did enjoy most of Taleworlds&apos; games, but have not been actively looking for anything new in that genre.

I bought Cinders right after Solstice, and bought Solstice mostly because I admired the work clearly put into the artwork. Enough to take a &quot;two Starbucks coffee&quot; risk on a game, anyway. The storytelling of Solstice was enough to buy Cinders in turn.

I was not disappointed. While the game formula ends up being a few choice-making points in the game (the actual number varies depending on your selection), this is mostly a game based on reading. If you are bothered by having to read dialogues in RPG games... well, this probably is not a good pick for you.

The storytelling is excellent. The characters are well crafted, and the ability to see several of their sides depending on your choices along made me replay the game twice immediately after finishing it for the first time. I absolutely love the graphical presentation (the amount of details that went into each background scene genuinely deserves calling them a work of art). There are enough plot twists to keep one interested (especially in alternative playthroughs), and it&apos;s a solid and fitting re-imagining of the old folk tale.

My only disappointment was the (understandably, from technical point of view) limited amount of branching. I&apos;d love to see even more possibilities, but even without those I already found Cinders experience highly engaging and pleasant.

Though I refuse to play for the &quot;bad&quot; endings. I came to like the characters too much.

Definitely recommend for people who enjoy reading, a well crafted plot and strong characterization, even if a single playthrough is a rather short experience.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1548d574-c3f5-11e8-b931-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A gorgeous game for bookworms</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">20</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/155642d6-9448-11e8-beac-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/155642d6-9448-11e8-beac-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2010-09-24T10:28:09</review:date>
        <review:description>Freespace 2 is, in my opinion, one of the defining games of the whole space combat genre. You play as a pilot in a little spaceship, and get thrown into large battles, which get very intense very quickly, and induce some real &quot;oh craaaaap&quot; moments when capital ships enter the fray. The single player campaign is long and varied, and an editor is included for making your own scenarios. In addition, online multiplayer is also available, for when you want challaneging battles with humans. The modding scene is active, with many campaigns and total conversions released, and the FreeSpace Open Source Project improves the game with better graphics and mod support.

In short: Buy it.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>155642d6-9448-11e8-beac-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Buy this game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/16a1d674-2fc4-11e9-b6f0-00163e4e60cc -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/16a1d674-2fc4-11e9-b6f0-00163e4e60cc">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-02-13T21:18:05</review:date>
        <review:description>The game is highly underrated from my point of view. I grew up with classic racing games like the Lotus series or Jaguar XJ220 on the Amiga. It is not exactly the same experience, but very enjoyable. I failed the first few races, but then i learned how to drift. That is very necessary to win a race. Remapping the controller buttons from RT and LT to A and B is also recommended. Start drifting is easier with this setup for me. Like others mentioned, the third part of the tutorial is broken. It&apos;s not a big deal, but i hope the devs will fix this.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>16a1d674-2fc4-11e9-b6f0-00163e4e60cc</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Classic style racing</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/16d198c4-ed32-11e9-bb1d-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/16d198c4-ed32-11e9-bb1d-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-10-12T23:51:39</review:date>
        <review:description>Lucas Pope fait partie de cette frange de producteur que l&apos;on peut sans aucun doute possible qualifier d&apos;auteur. Proposer en 2019 un jeu dont les graphismes nous font retourner à la préhistoire des jeux vidéo, il y a 35 ans, est un pari risqué mais qu&apos;il remporte haut la main. Sans compter que cette technique sert le propos d&apos;enquête du jeu, nous limitant grandement dans nos observations, avec son absence de couleurs et sa simplicité de modélisation. Et si on peut cependant regretter un style graphique qui peut défoncer la rétine sur les longues sessions de jeu, force est de constater que je suis tombé sous le charme de ce style désuet mais totalement maîtrisé.

Outre cette technique sortie des annales du jeu vidéo, Obra Dinn se hisse également tout en haut du podium de jeu d&apos;enquête. Le core gameplay du jeu consiste à enquêter sur la mort des 60 passagers du navire à travers toute une série de scénettes immobiles sous forme de flashbacks. Nous pouvons nous promener à notre guise sur les lieux, à l&apos;instant précis de chacune des morts. Et on va devoir mettre un nom sur chacun de ces corps. On va ainsi comprendre que l&apos;Obra Dinn n&apos;a pas essuyé une seule et grande catastrophe ayant anéanti tout son équipage d&apos;un coup, mais qu&apos;il a plutôt enchaîné les péripéties assez violentes digne d&apos;une malédiction imprimant ses rouages à vif sur le front des passagers. Et sans trop en révéler, on peut d&apos;ores et déjà affirmer que Return of The Obra Dinn est le meilleur jeu lovecraftien de 2019, bien loin devant The Call of Cthulhu et The Sinking City.

En résumé, Obra Dinn est un jeu rare, qui parvient à nous surprendre car ce qu&apos;il nous propose est absolument maîtrisé, et il nous fait aussi nous surprendre nous-même, réussissant l&apos;exploit de nous faire croire intelligent.
Un délice, mangez-en !</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>16d198c4-ed32-11e9-bb1d-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Le meilleur jeu d&apos;enquête ever</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/16da9464-f111-11e9-8442-00163e97122a -->

