    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1632e440-d07a-11eb-9822-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/1632e440-d07a-11eb-9822-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-06-19T00:13:54</review:date>
        <review:description>Since it doesn&apos;t seem like GOG offers a text box to just say why I want to refund the game, I&apos;ll just post it here.

I downloaded the game using the &quot;backup installer&quot; method like I would do with any other GOG game. I don&apos;t want / need Galaxy. After installing the game the first thing I notice is that multiplayer is grayed out completely. It states the game requires an internet connection for this to be available. After some quick searching around this game requires Galaxy to function, and obviously must also require internet access to play it in MP.

Shame on GOG for even allowing this item on the store. &quot;We are GOG.COM, the DRM Free
home for a Curated selection of games. This place was made for gamers, so make yourself at home. &quot; If you want to keep touting that on the home page, then games like this just need to be removed until they clean up their act and actually implement a DRM free game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">45</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>1632e440-d07a-11eb-9822-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Not DRM Free</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">39</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/167f613e-4462-11eb-af0c-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/167f613e-4462-11eb-af0c-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-12-22T16:29:24</review:date>
        <review:description>This game hits the mark on many levels. Background music is orchestral, tense, and there&apos;s enough of it to keep from being too repetitive. The pixel art works well. Scenes and levels are creepy. The intro is very strong, and kept me wanting to learn more for an hour or so.

But it felt like there were too many doors to go through, too much backtracking, and a couple of undiscoverable things like &quot;hit the invisible red button in the shadows to scare the psycho killer.&quot; As with most adventure games, I had to open a walkthrough to keep progressing.

I think the biggest flaw was progression: it didn&apos;t feel like I was getting closer to a solution or understanding as the game went on.

I made it about 75% through, then watched a longplay video for the rest.  Was easily worth a few dollars for a good set-up and good artwork. The soundtrack is recommended if you like horror movie music.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>167f613e-4462-11eb-af0c-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Creepy, atmospheric. Good horror music.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


