    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/17cf5fca-c995-11e8-a51f-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/17cf5fca-c995-11e8-a51f-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-10-06T21:24:42</review:date>
        <review:description>A nice little title with a decent difficulty curve. While it doesn&apos;t join the ranks of the best of the genre (see Hollow Knight), it is certainly worth your time if you love Castlevania types (SotN).

Combat is good. It&apos;s a little clunky, but not badly enough that you&apos;ll be frustrated at it. Dodging/rolling is skillfully is required to defeat the bosses, and knowing when to attack, and when to retreat.
Attacking roots you for a moment (which feels outdated), and some bosses can be cheesed using ranged spam.

The upgrade system (passive charms and active items) could have been more interesting, but function well enough.

Level design is mostly good. Nice platforming, interesting enemy placement. Decent secret exploration. The devs did a good job including world building (NPCs you can talk to, and interesting environments which tell the story).

Artistically the game excels. It goes for an anime-ish art direction (Sailor Moon), and pulls it off well. I thought the game would be more fan-servicey (main character with short skirt and high red stockings, and the witch of decay with you attacking her boobs more or less), but it really doesn&apos;t devolve into tastelessness (I do like some fan service in my games, just not when it&apos;s totally shameless. This game definitely could have even used more, but it&apos;s fine the way it is).

Music is ultimately forgettable. When there is some sort of melody, it&apos;s not good. When it&apos;s going for atmosphere, it really does well (heavy bass, sense of dread type stuff).

Overall, this game is worth your time if you are a fan of this type of game. I enjoyed it and 100%&apos;d the map -- definitely don&apos;t regret playing it. However, it falls in the middle of the pack, so if you&apos;re on a budget, look at some of the other ones first -- but do eventually come back to this one. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>17cf5fca-c995-11e8-a51f-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>A Good (but not Great) game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/17ef050a-bc1e-11ea-b4b4-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/17ef050a-bc1e-11ea-b4b4-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-07-02T07:40:02</review:date>
        <review:description>Quick note: Highly recommend playing with a friend (if neither of you is a control freak).

The Return of Obra Dinn makes you feel like a real detective going on context clues and using knowledge about the world. It&apos;s one of those games that&apos;s not addicting but is so good you can&apos;t help thinking about it offline as you piece together the puzzle of the ship.

Usually when playing a new game, I&apos;ve already played a game like it and can compare. TROOD on the other hand stands completely apart. For avid gamers it gives you that treasured &apos;first time&apos; feeling (like playing ocarina of time did for me as a kid). For beginners, the mechanics are simple enough to understand and enjoyable even if you don&apos;t know the common language of video games.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>17ef050a-bc1e-11ea-b4b4-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>In a genre of its own</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/173a1118-d9a2-11e9-b0d5-c2651dc5f937 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/173a1118-d9a2-11e9-b0d5-c2651dc5f937">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-09-17T23:23:00</review:date>
        <review:description>Tam gdzie Lucyfer moze, czyli plusy:

+ walka; bardzo dynamiczna, trudna i wymagajaca
+ drzewko rozwojowe; wyglada przezroczyscie, latwe w obsludze, mozna odznaczac i ponownie wybrac zdolnosci
+ fabula; dosc prosta aczkolwiek przyjemna, nasza postac jest zla i sie z tym nie ukrywa

Tam gdzie Lucyfera reka nie siega, czyli minusy:
- dosc prosta oprawa graficzna
- animacja postaci
- niedokonca przetlumaczone tresci angielskie (listy,pocztowki, dokumenty)

Nie zapominajmy, ze to debiut nowego polskiego studia!</review:description>
