    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2b33dc14-6357-11ea-8b6a-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2b33dc14-6357-11ea-8b6a-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-03-11T07:14:22</review:date>
        <review:description>One of the most immersive, interesting and fresh experiences in gaming that I ever had. A breakthrough in terms of visual effects, old-school gaming, with a perfect balance of great voice acting and perfect storytelling.

This game is a pinnacle of &quot;show, don&apos;t tell&quot; examples and it&apos;s amazing how it can keep you engaged without ever giving you animated scenes at all during the investigation. A game like this would never be possible back in the day of its origins, but it would never be possible today without the influence of the old monitor aesthetics. And god, the surprises are awesome.

I can&apos;t recommend this enough. It&apos;s hard to explain but with half an hour of gameplay you will surely know what I mean. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>2b33dc14-6357-11ea-8b6a-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>No words to truly describe this...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2b4c7a54-5973-11eb-b12c-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2b4c7a54-5973-11eb-b12c-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-01-18T11:54:34</review:date>
        <review:description>I don&apos;t like survival simulators much — they seem pointless and grindy to me (I don&apos;t like Factorio for example).

This one is epic  — it combines base building, people management (think Majesty or The Sims), resource management and crafting, good bit of automation (only when required), on demand direct control mechanics, procedure-generated world and veeery nice stories that emerge around all the characters in the game.
Losing a character or a pet makes me sad as it would have been when reading the book — I&apos;m rarely as such compassionate to game characters (randomly generated characters!).

I&apos;m doing my first run and am now in the mid-late game (first spaceship techs and mechanoids) and still I&apos;m not bored while playing — usually I feel like I&apos;m grinding in a couple of days of play.
I&apos;m start to lack overall goals in the game though — I guess I need to increase difficulty (which works like a charm here) or start playing from scratch in a different scenario.

</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>2b4c7a54-5973-11eb-b12c-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Very nice survival with good stories</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2b58b5f4-dfcf-11e9-a59e-22399ab0a1d4 -->

