    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2ee0a736-952f-11e8-9b98-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2ee0a736-952f-11e8-9b98-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2014-03-15T22:33:28</review:date>
        <review:description>This is the best space combat game I&apos;ve ever had the joy of playing, and with the Source Code Project you can get this looking like a modern game quite easily. 

First thing you have to know is that this does not use realistic physics, it&apos;s more like WW2 dogfighting in space, or what you&apos;d see in Star Wars.

The combat feels great, especially with a joystick. Ships aren&apos;t too durable, so diving into a pack of fighters is both dangerous and potentially rewarding. Unlike Freespace 1 warships can be very deadly. You do NOT want to be pulled into a dogfight within range of anti-fighter beams. Ships, especially big ships, also make a very satisfying boom when they come apart into chunks of burning metal and pretty shockwaves.

The story is also great, constantly building you up just to tear you down again. You&apos;ll have moments that make you go &quot;Hell Yeah!&quot; usually followed by an &quot;Oh S***....&quot; moment. Freespace 2 definitely deserved a sequel, but the ending also reinforces the threat of the main villains. They aren&apos;t cackling evil aliens threatening you with lame taunts. They never speak to you, and you never really get a grasp on exactly what they want while still being able to hint that they do in fact HAVE motives. They almost seem like a force of nature more than an opposing faction.

If you want a space combat game that makes you feel like being part of galactic war, this is something you must try.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>2ee0a736-952f-11e8-9b98-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>My favorite game of all time.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2eef38f2-e5b2-11ea-a59b-fa163e7ab9c9 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2eef38f2-e5b2-11ea-a59b-fa163e7ab9c9">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-08-24T05:33:24</review:date>
        <review:description>Bought this on sale just on a whim, and I&apos;ve really enjoyed it.  I&apos;m a huge fan of Warcraft 3, Starcraft and the several of the Warhammer 40K series, and this one has its on special place in my heart.

In a lot of ways the resource management is much more complicated than the others, which is a nice change for me.  The single player story mode is fun and has various challenges to keep it interesting.

Looking forward to multiplayer, but I&apos;m not seeing a LAN option.  I hope the bandwidth requirements are not too high as I&apos;m limited by a Dumb Slow Line (DSL).  I haven&apos;t checked out any of the other DLC yet, but might if multiplayer goes well.

Oddly enough, it doesn&apos;t seem to have a lot in the way of graphics configuration.  I noticed that there was no option to set my default resolution if I wanted to back it down.  This seemed odd to me.

The graphic style is okay, but is a little more cartoon-ish than I&apos;m used to, but it is good.  I&apos;ve gotten spoiled by the Divinity Original Sin series.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>2eef38f2-e5b2-11ea-a59b-fa163e7ab9c9</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Fun Little RTS</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2eefcc74-8bd8-11ea-88c6-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2eefcc74-8bd8-11ea-88c6-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-05-01T21:18:40</review:date>
        <review:description>Delta Force is supposed to be a game where you control a squad in order to stealthily overtake an enemy base or extract captured civilians, but the game is so clunkily designed, it&apos;s much quicker to run and gun.
Enemy AI is almost non-existent, so you have only two types of enemies - ones that fire non-stop even if there&apos;s no target to fire at, giving away their location, and enemies that camp and wait for you to stare them in the face before they start firing, and even then it takes them a second or two before they realize they ought to open fire upon you.

They do sometimes come off as intelligent, when at times it seems like they&apos;re trying to flank you, but their path selection isn&apos;t very good, causing them to either take a long detour and to never be seen again, or to climb up a hill you&apos;re on head on, making themselves easy targets.

It seems the game compensates by letting them know of your whereabouts at all times, regardless of if you&apos;re under cover or not. It&apos;s better to have cover, but enemies seem to sometimes spot you through hills, and fire at you as if those hills aren&apos;t there.
If you do pop out of cover, it seems they go through the same motions of firing near you at a fixed distance, firing again much nearer to you, again at a fixed distance, and only then hitting you.

Yet other times just walking around stealthily, you&apos;ll be hit by a sniper&apos;s bullet - a sniper that somehow saw you, and managed to kill you with his first bullet, even when you&apos;re a moving target.
It puts an end to stealth, and forces you to do the same - get a gun with a good scope and plenty of ammo, and scour the level for enemies.
After a while you can distinguish with a naked eye between a shrub and an enemy, both about 4 pixels big.

There are plenty of weapons, but the best gun is a gun with single &amp;amp; burst fire, and a scope.
Running and gunning makes missions blend together, and I could have played dozens more, but stealth is too time consuming and somewhat broken.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>2eefcc74-8bd8-11ea-88c6-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>A fun failed game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2ef0bb8e-a9cd-11eb-a01f-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/2ef0bb8e-a9cd-11eb-a01f-fa163e99870e">
