        <review:title>Meh</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">70</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/33dbeb60-f202-11ea-8aa4-fa163e93294d -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/33dbeb60-f202-11ea-8aa4-fa163e93294d">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-09-08T21:36:26</review:date>
        <review:description>Kingdoms of Amalur has always been one of my favorite RPGs. So, when I caught wind of it getting a DRM-free release on GOG, I was over the moon. Now this is supposed to be the &quot;remastered&quot; version, but I have to say - I have not noticed a lot of difference between this and the original.

A few good things. KoA has a nice and fairly open world, which can be explored at your own pace. There are dungeons and quests to find off the beaten path. There is a nice selection of weapons to arm your character with, some of them are quite unique (i.e. faeblades, chakrams). The character development is fairly simple, and by the end of the game you may have gotten most of the &quot;passive&quot; skills. There is a fairly nice selection of abilities to chose from, both magic and melee. You may always respec your character at any point if you change your mind in regard to your abilities or passives.

There are a lot of quests, though a lot of them are pretty basic. The areas of the map are varied. The voice acting is alright, you may even recognize some voice actors from other games such as Skyrim or New Vegas.

It ran pretty well on my machine, which is quite old at this point. I only encountered severe frame drops when using the &quot;arrow rain&quot; skill, for some reason and not always, but nothing too bad, in my opinion. As of today (09/07/2021) the game does support Galaxy achievements, but does not support the Galaxy overlay, so there is no satisfying sound and notification when you pop an achievement (most likely subject to change if the Galaxy is not abandoned). The game is fairly long, my first GOG playthrough took me around 54 hours (and I honestly have not completed all of the side quests, as by the second DLC I had maxed out and become overpowered, aaaaand burnt-out a bit). To sum up, an amazing game, perhaps not the most mature or dark, but still fun and interesting. Strongly recommended.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>33dbeb60-f202-11ea-8aa4-fa163e93294d</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A standard edition review</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">23</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/33f4c1b4-793c-11eb-a336-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/33f4c1b4-793c-11eb-a336-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-02-27T22:41:44</review:date>
        <review:description>I just finished my 2nd plathrough of Pathologic 2 and I must say I kinda fell in love with the game, having played 65 hours over the course of 2 weeks.

The game offers an absolutely unique gaming experience. Survival, healing and storyline come together in a truly wonderful way, not to mention the superb soundtrack and a nice graphic design. Many games attempt to be philosophical, but pathologic offers its insight in a very natural and mature way. First game in a long time that actually made me think deeply about who I was and how I wanted to progress.

The difficulty is high, but manageable and the game offers enough time and resources to make it through. I found that there was sufficient room for error, even on the &quot;intented&quot; difficulty.

I experienced a few minor glitches, but restarting the game always helped. I also wish that the tutorial had explained the more advanced mechanics as well, not just the basics of survival and healing. Other than that, a wonderful game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>33f4c1b4-793c-11eb-a336-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Mesmerizing, original</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/330d6836-a761-11e9-9afb-7a6232092033 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/330d6836-a761-11e9-9afb-7a6232092033">
