    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/34fc25aa-93f9-11e8-a123-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/34fc25aa-93f9-11e8-a123-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2008-09-14T07:20:04</review:date>
        <review:description>Good graphics(for the time), good gameplay and good music. If you havent played it, buy it!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>34fc25aa-93f9-11e8-a123-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>One of the best games i have played</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/34329572-b1d2-11e8-91d8-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/34329572-b1d2-11e8-91d8-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2016-08-13T18:53:46</review:date>
        <review:description>First of all I need to admit that i only played around 2-3 hours of game. But those couple of hours gave me a good glimpse on this game i think.
Main problem is that it is just boring. You build buildings to make units faster. Those units die quick, so you better tap those keyboard button shourcuts fast.  Rence and repeat, untill you kill all enemy units.
It&apos;s hard to implement any strategy as your units are braindead when you command them. When you click &apos;A&apos; (attack) behind enemy lines, they will scatter and just do what they want, dying in the process of course. As only one building of same kind can produce, there is no way to split your base, so no strategy here either.
Story is non-existent, there is few sentences in mission select screen but that&apos;s all.
As there where no good AAA RTS release for like a decade (maybe except CoH and Homeworld remake) it&apos;s sad that smaller devs are only making those useless cookie-clikers.

Overall, it&apos;s boring, dummed down Warcraft 2, set in mobile games mindset of simple repetative chore.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">39</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>34329572-b1d2-11e8-91d8-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Boring &amp;amp; repetetive disappointment</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">63</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/344abec8-c42a-11eb-86bf-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/344abec8-c42a-11eb-86bf-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-06-03T08:11:51</review:date>
        <review:description>A game that requires heavy decision making. You will not feel like the unaffected super hero that can save everybody and accomplish everything. Instead the game humbles you into feeling the real desperation of your situation. If you ever find yourself rolling your eyes at the crazy feats the MC are able to accomplish in other games then this is your jam. There is a steep learning curve alongside some dialogue that might require you to reread a couple times to truly understand its meaning but a game that I no doubt recommend as someone who plays every genre and is very nitpicky. hope you enjoy! </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>344abec8-c42a-11eb-86bf-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Real game lovers should play</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/34440df2-111b-11eb-85c3-fa163edf6d96 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/34440df2-111b-11eb-85c3-fa163edf6d96">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-10-18T11:23:30</review:date>
        <review:description>Skyrim is the easiest comparison. Amalur has better exploration, a lot less bug (doesn&apos;t mean no bug, try to manual save every hour or half hour in case of quest breaking bug), a splendid pacing with quests and exploration, a pretty competent medfan story with interesting oddities (especially the emphasis on the fae), very satisfying combat and character progression.
This game is best enjoyed with a nice podcast during the exploration phases.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">17</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>34440df2-111b-11eb-85c3-fa163edf6d96</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>A better Skyrim</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">9</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3448e5ce-946e-11e8-900a-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3448e5ce-946e-11e8-900a-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2011-06-23T11:18:37</review:date>
        <review:description>...for me the answer would be NO. I was too young when the game was released and so I passed it and lived over a decade whiteout hearing anything about it. With the summer &quot;Staycation&quot; promo from gog 2011 I took the risk of 3$ and bought the game. After I fixed the texture bug with the help of the AWESOME gog.com community I was ready to enjoy the game and what a game it is! After all this years I found in MDK the game of my dreams: pure third person action. If you ask me anybody who likes jumping and shooting at the same time should buy this game it is that good. Thank you gog for this mind blowing  experience ;)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>3448e5ce-946e-11e8-900a-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Have you ever heard of MDK?</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/349c220e-93fe-11e8-b282-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/349c220e-93fe-11e8-b282-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2009-07-04T21:10:10</review:date>
        <review:description>10 Years ago there was a genre of games that was quite popular amongst us gamers. The Space Sims were plentiful and we had a veritable pantheon of great games to choose from. The days of Tie Fighter, X-Wing and Privateer are held dearly in my heart. Volition, the developers of Descent broke new ground with their hit title Descent: Freespace. Freespace gave us great graphics, great performance and some fantastic gameplay. The game sold rather well and Volition emmidiately started work on a sequel.

It wasn&apos;t long until Freespace 2 came out. Freespace 2 improved every aspect of the first game right down to the Multiplayer. Amazing visuals, a great set of ships and weapons and we loved it. Freespace 2 is one of the last games of a dying genre to be released. With community support Freespace 2 still holds up today as a fantastic game. Get this game, download FS2Open and enjoy one of the best there is.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>349c220e-93fe-11e8-b282-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A long lost genre&apos;s last words.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/349e5b0e-b2b3-11e8-b404-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/349e5b0e-b2b3-11e8-b404-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-03-18T06:57:00</review:date>
        <review:description>I don&apos;t own this game, I just tried it at a friend&apos;s computer this afternoon.

TL;DR: don&apos;t buy this game if you haven&apos;t played the demo, controls and how the game makes you use them are horrible.

First of all, there&apos;s some horror in here. It feels like a graphic adventure, but instead of point-and-click, it&apos;s move-and-press-E. And not in a 2D scenery, but in one dimensional scenery, moving left and right. There&apos;s no mouse support, no gamepad support, and not only the controls are 90% at the left hand (that&apos;s why I say it&apos;s a game for left-handed), but as the other reviewer has already said, you can&apos;t redefine the keys, and what&apos;s worse, there&apos;s some kind of irregular choice about when to use escape, the space bar, or the E key to continue, like are you examining a letter, finishing/continuing a dialog or description (which aren&apos;t autoclosed at move, you must close them manually and it&apos;s not always clear which of the keys can be used.

WASD for movement (never used WS for the short time I played), plus E and F, and then the far I for inventory... And a clumsy, counterintuitive and very unnatural way to interact with stuff, make playing this game an annoying experience. I can&apos;t vouch for the game itself, as I wasn&apos;t able to stand the controls and how the game wants you to use them anymore. The sounds and graphics are ok, maybe somewhere there&apos;s a decent game to be played, but with this control scheme I really can&apos;t imagine myself wanting to try ever again.

I&apos;m glad I didn&apos;t waste my money on this one (for my friend), but I&apos;m sorry for my friend because the horrid controls. Try the demo if you want to test yourself.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">22</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>349e5b0e-b2b3-11e8-b404-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>A game for left-handed people</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">17</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35a915be-c584-11e8-964c-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35a915be-c584-11e8-964c-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2011-07-04T00:52:00</review:date>
        <review:description>First of all, this game&apos;s graphics have aged very well.  The lasers glow, the rockets fwoosh, the big ships slash through each other with massive beam cannons and spray flak and lasers everywhere.  It still looks good after all these years, and there are texture packs online to improve it even more.  All the races have a distinct style: The Terrans get standard blocky ships, the Vasudans get organic yellow curves, and the Shivans get scary twisted black and red.  These graphics make it amazingly satisfying to dogfight or help out in a big capship battle.

The gameplay is fairly straightforward: There&apos;s a nice variety of missions, from dogfighting to interception to escort, and they&apos;re all pretty fun.  Dogfighting handles nicely since the game lets you automatically match speeds to stay on your enemy&apos;s tail, gives you a reticle to lead your target, and other helpful bits of information on your HUD.  Dogfighting, chucking missiles around, divebombing a massive capship to demolish its beam cannons and give your own capitals a fighting chance, these are all tons of fun and the main reason I gave this game 4 stars.

Unfortunately, the game&apos;s more advanced controls have more keys than a pipe organ.  There&apos;s like 10 different targeting options, from &quot;target the next enemy&quot; to &quot;target the ship subsystem under my reticle,&quot; each with its own key, your guns have different firing modes and that gets a few more keys, your throttle settings take up all your number keys and more to make adjustments, and you get miscellaneous keys for your flares, shield orientation, and so on.  It&apos;s a mess.  If you find you need to switch guns mid-battle, you&apos;ll definitely have to take your eyes off the screen for a while.

Also, bomb interception is the most annoying game type ever invented.  The idea is that when an enemy bomber tries to torpedo your capship, you can shoot down their bomb before it hits, then hunt down the bomber before they fire again.  Unfortunately, bombs are hard to spot, so you don&apos;t always know when you should be running interception and when you should be hunting bombers (strike one).  Then you need to press the &quot;B&quot; key to actually target the bomb, one of those many confusing keyboard commands I mentioned above (strike two), and bombs travel pretty fast so you usually won&apos;t intercept them in time, anyway (strike three!).  I just gave up and let my wingmen handle it.

So, beautiful graphics, varied gameplay, exciting dogfights, massive battleship duels, marred by messy controls and annoying bombers.  Definitely worth getting, just be aware of these issues before you jump in.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35a915be-c584-11e8-964c-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Beautiful and exciting, but not perfect</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35b8dce0-93fe-11e8-8a88-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35b8dce0-93fe-11e8-8a88-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2009-07-05T12:52:50</review:date>
        <review:description>The concept behind Lionheart - that of a world changed by magic and the influx of spirits, is a really well-fleshed out concept by the designers. They created a beautiful history, plausible nations and factions...

...and then they proceeded to create 1/3rd of a great game, and 2/3rds of a game that was obviously rushed out the door.

The game play is pretty good (although ranged attacks are relatively disadvantaged due to the layout), and the first chapter (in Spain) is very well designed, full of subplots, good dialogue, and character choices.

Once you complete the chapter in Spain, the game goes downhill quickly. The area in France is underdeveloped, but still has decent subplots at first - it then devolves into a straight line sprint to the end. Because of the way the game quickly devolves into a boring mess, you&apos;ll find yourself quickly losing interest after the first few areas in France.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35b8dce0-93fe-11e8-8a88-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Great concept - rushed delivery</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35cfa50a-3a8d-11eb-aa31-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35cfa50a-3a8d-11eb-aa31-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-12-10T04:12:53</review:date>
        <review:description>Played this on Ubuntu Mate 18.04. Great little game with a lot of heart. Not my usual cup of tea but I liked this games humor, story line and art work. If you&apos;re looking for a short sci-fi adventure game with a touch of horror, this is a good buy.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35cfa50a-3a8d-11eb-aa31-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Short. Linear. Sweet.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35de356c-93f9-11e8-96c5-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35de356c-93f9-11e8-96c5-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2008-09-14T18:28:40</review:date>
        <review:description>This is the most important thing to keep in mind. As I didn&apos;t work on the project, it&apos;s impossible for me to say without a doubt how much Black Isle contributed to the development of the game, but the primary work was done by Reflexive Entertainment, probably best known today for their independent game distribution site. At first glance one could be fooled into thinking that it is in fact a genuine Black Isle game. It has a promising alternate historical setting with a fantasy twist, and adapts the wonderful SPECIAL character development system from Fallout to its milieu (and unfortunately real-time combat). Furthermore, the opening city has lots of interesting quests to do, historical figures to meet, and so on. Sadly, after the plot takes you outside that city&apos;s walls, the game falls apart and becomes little more than a rather limp Diablo clone. Shame, really.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">11</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35de356c-93f9-11e8-96c5-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Published, not developed by Black Isle</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">73</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35e57644-08eb-11e9-8c42-fa163ecdd5fb -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35e57644-08eb-11e9-8c42-fa163ecdd5fb">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-12-26T10:49:52</review:date>
        <review:description>I played the game together with my wife and we had a wonderful time. We finished it after almost 17 hours. After playing a lot of murder/puzzle games this one really stands out. 

I think the game mechanics are very well explained in the description. You visit different scenes and try to assign the name and the fate of each crew member and passenger with some bits of audio dialogue and a still image. Just make sure to observe the scene carefully :)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35e57644-08eb-11e9-8c42-fa163ecdd5fb</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Wonderful game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/350b1f5a-f947-11ea-ac0d-fa163e7ab9c9 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/350b1f5a-f947-11ea-ac0d-fa163e7ab9c9">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-09-18T03:38:01</review:date>
        <review:description>Short random levels, random loot, die -&amp;gt; start from the very beginning
You should shoot your weapon in harmony with the music beats (but you can turn it off).

Cons? Weird graphics, some unfair moments (I did not like to be killed by a boss on his loading show up).
Pros? Awesome gameplay, music. And no issues with ammo! (but weapon reloading also should be synced with music).

One of the best indies of this year imo.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>350b1f5a-f947-11ea-ac0d-fa163e7ab9c9</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>When Doom meets Crypt of the Necrodancer</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/350f9e46-93f9-11e8-ba6f-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/350f9e46-93f9-11e8-ba6f-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2008-09-20T11:49:36</review:date>
        <review:description>I&apos;d never played the game until buying it from GoG, but remembered hearing  many people fondly reminiscing about it over the years.  After all these years, could it possibly live up to the hype...?

Oh my god YES!

The Aliens, the explosions, the collosal starships, swarms of small fighters nipping through space lit up by glowing capital ship beam lasers and flak-cannon fire (now I know where the new Battlestar Galactica got the effect from!), clusters of missile trails weaving after their prey like schools of piranha...Freespace 2 offers the most wonderfully realised and breathtaking space combat ever coded. Your fighter/bomber handles like a dream (even with a mouse!), and in no time at all you&apos;ll be defending freighters and attacking cap ships with gleeful whoops of joy. This game cannot be recommend often enough or highly enough; simply UNNMISSABLE,

PS: As a default install she looks fine, but you have the option of bringing her graphics into the 21st century using the FS_Open Modding project, which can be found at www.hard-light.net. New models, new textures, and all running beautifully at modern resolutions!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">23</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>350f9e46-93f9-11e8-ba6f-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>The Pinnacle of Space Combat</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">872</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3502b41a-93f9-11e8-be34-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3502b41a-93f9-11e8-be34-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2008-09-14T08:40:52</review:date>
        <review:description>As someone seeing it with fresh eyes, this is a pretty dismal game, with horrible immersion.  The levels don&apos;t feel even vaguely real; they&apos;re just organized set pieces to make you do things in a particular order.  The interface is remarkably awkward for a shooter, and they don&apos;t really explain anything, leaving you floundering.  

I mean, the whole idea of the game is that you possess other creatures, and have them do your bidding, right?  They DON&apos;T EVEN TELL YOU HOW TO POSSESS CREATURES.  (You have to jump into them from the back.)  This is probably partially my fault for not reading the manual, and perhaps I&apos;ll get more out of it after doing that, but if you just sit down and play without finding outside help, you&apos;re likely to be extremely frustrated.  

Further, the controls are really strange.  It&apos;s hard to aim where you want to, it&apos;s hard to shoot, it&apos;s hard to move.  Basically, in every way imaginable, it&apos;s just awkward as hell, and frustrating.  

On top of THAT, your possessed character has an &apos;aggressive&apos; mode and a &apos;non-aggressive&apos; mode; you stay in the aggressive mode after shooting until you hit your use button.  ANYONE WHO SEES YOU in attack mode (including your putative subordinates, after you take over a commander) will immediately turn hostile and start attacking.  SHOOTING doesn&apos;t make them hostile, just having your gun up does.  This is extremely frustrating and stupid, and it was this one decision alone that soured me on this game. 

Overall, I&apos;d say to avoid it.  It&apos;s just not very good.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">21</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>3502b41a-93f9-11e8-be34-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Not worthless, but...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">18</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35120864-9496-11e8-be1e-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35120864-9496-11e8-be1e-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2011-12-24T02:44:59</review:date>
        <review:description>Played Freespace 1 years ago and felt it was a real treat - and bought 1+2 on this site.

To my surprise, the graphics still hold up - sure the game doesn&apos;t have bump mappng and parallax mapping and all that goodness - but the models are nice and the only time you might feel the graphics lacking is on the big ships where the texture is not so pretty.

But besides that - the small ships look ok, the explosions are great, the effects are great - the atmosphere and gameplay is awesome!

But if this review put you down on the graphics side - I woyuld still want you to try it out - why?

Because the Indie-scene isn&apos;t that much better alot of the games graphic wise, but they argue instead for better feeling, atmosphere and so on - if you are an indie game person then you must give this space sim classic, made by a big studio, a chance!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35120864-9496-11e8-be1e-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Still holds up!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/352bed3c-b256-11e8-935e-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/352bed3c-b256-11e8-935e-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2017-08-07T05:56:55</review:date>
        <review:description>I don&apos;t know how I missed this game, but I&apos;m glad I found it now. If you loved Elite, the original, Colony Wars and X-wing vs Tie Fighter then you will love this game. Even though it was written in 1999 it has aged remarkably well, but if you want to have the ultimate FS2 experience you have to checkout Freespace Open. The 2014 mod makes this game like watching a TV show. Epic space battles that make you feel like you&apos;re in an episode of Babylon 5.

Be warned though, while you can play with a mouse, I highly recommend you get a flight stick, it adds an even deeper level of immersion to an already incredibly atmospheric game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>352bed3c-b256-11e8-935e-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Epic Space Opera</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3539bf80-6686-11ea-a953-fa163e93294d -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3539bf80-6686-11ea-a953-fa163e93294d">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-03-15T08:28:39</review:date>
        <review:description>I&apos;ll keep it short...  I have over 500 hours since I purchased this game a year ago.  It&apos;s one of my top games of all time.  This game is dangerous to play on a work night, because the hours slip away, and you don&apos;t even realize it.  Checkout the gameplay vids on the youtubes, and see for yourself why fancy graphics don&apos;t define a game.  This one will go down in history as one of the greats!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>3539bf80-6686-11ea-a953-fa163e93294d</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Have to add my 5-star</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35489fc4-9585-11e8-98b1-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35489fc4-9585-11e8-98b1-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2015-04-25T16:03:24</review:date>
        <review:description>Even now, some 16 years later, this is truly an astonishing piece of gaming history. Beautiful graphics, amazing gameplay and a difficulty setting that is challenging without being impossible.
I remember playing the hatful of sci-fi space warring games which were common back &quot;in the day&quot;, The X-wing series, Wing commander et al, all brilliant, but this is the true standout genius, standing at the pinnacle of joystick battering glory.
Buy it, you&apos;re a fool if you don&apos;t!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35489fc4-9585-11e8-98b1-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Genius</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35721db0-3559-11eb-a979-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/35721db0-3559-11eb-a979-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-12-03T13:18:03</review:date>
        <review:description>More of the same just with added difficulty in certain areas, super enjoyable with a friend.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>35721db0-3559-11eb-a979-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>More Outward</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10</review:upvotes>
