    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3719d5a8-e63b-11ea-8863-fa163edf6d96 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3719d5a8-e63b-11ea-8863-fa163edf6d96">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-08-24T21:54:19</review:date>
        <review:description>This game is different. This is something I&apos;ve never played before. I am not into point and click adventures but what I&apos;ve read about it... Just found it interesting...
400 days to wait... 400 days to spend underground to wake up the king... the king who needs to regain his power, to end all fear and longing...

Shade is an interesting character. A lonely one who you can relate. The game is extremely slow! But... I could accept that and since time passes even when the game is closed... you can just return to it later. But there are activities to do, to spend some time... The atmosphere is one of the game&apos;s strength. Exploring the cave system is nice. Finding new places is exciting. Finding dead ends too. You know, after a while, you can overcome the challenge. The world is changing, you just have to wait. 
Decorating your little home is fun. 

The game is somewhat depressing. At the same time, helping Shade in his mission to wait, makes me feel better. The game is about patience, appreciating small successes. It teaches you something. What&apos;s the end of the game? What happens after 400 days? I don&apos;t know and I don&apos;t want to know! I don&apos;t want to watch videos... I wanna experience it all my self.

The game is not for everyone. It&apos;s a special kind of game. Unique, strange, lonely, full of soul and charm. Nice hand drawn artwork, ambient music and sounds...
Interesting and captivating. 

Bought it on Steam, sadly I just realized it&apos;s on GOG too... oh well... maybe I&apos;ll buy it here too... after a long 400 days...</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>3719d5a8-e63b-11ea-8863-fa163edf6d96</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>So special...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/372fc8d2-871c-11ea-9413-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/372fc8d2-871c-11ea-9413-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-04-25T20:43:04</review:date>
        <review:description>To be honest, I haven&apos;t even tried the retail vanilla FS2 after I bought it. Nostalgia-coloured glasses is not my preferable fashion choice. What I did instead was gping over to the Hardlight forums, get the Knossos mod manager, download half a dozen of most popular mods and overhauls, and start kicking butts. 

One disclaimer: FS2. for all the arcade-ness, is pretty simulationist when it comes to controls. And with all the craziness on the battlefield you will want to run your fighter as smooth as possible.

Fortunately, you can re-map all the keys, and you better do it. If you don&apos;t have a stick, base your movement controls (pitch/turn) on the WSAD-cross, with acceleration nearby, a dozen or so other useful keys as you like them, ignore all the other controls and do the fine-aiming and firing with your mouse- and you&apos;re gonna be dandy. It is a pretty steep learning curve, but once you get rollin, it&apos;s fun AF. 

Everyone else already gushed about how amazing the game is. Space sims don&apos;t get any better than this (and I loved both TIE Fighter and Wing Commander). The mods and overhauls will make the game look like it came out... well, maybe not yesterday, but circa 2010, and a lot of the fan content could pass for AAA products, no sweat.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>372fc8d2-871c-11ea-9413-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Believe the hype</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/37207fbc-a74c-11ea-93fb-fa163e93294d -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/37207fbc-a74c-11ea-93fb-fa163e93294d">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-06-05T19:47:17</review:date>
        <review:description>The art style is fantastic (I recommend play on LCD display setting)
The story, acting and writing are fantastic
The gameplay mechanic itself is first one ever to make me feel like I&apos;m actually an investigator.

Took me several evening to beat - about 12 hours total, but I say it was worth every cent</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>37207fbc-a74c-11ea-93fb-fa163e93294d</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A masterpiece</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/37237806-93f9-11e8-a4f3-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/37237806-93f9-11e8-a4f3-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2008-09-15T20:19:03</review:date>
        <review:description>It&apos;s a simple thing to say, but Freespace 2 truly is the best game of its genre. There hasn&apos;t been a space combat sim since that&apos;s truly captured the atmosphere and epic scale of the combat. The gameplay model is just the right side of &quot;simulation&quot; to give it some real depth, whilst being accessible to newcomers, and for once, your wingmen are actually useful (giving your wingmen orders is ESSENTIAL in order to be able to survive the later missions). The combat is fast paced but deliberate, with much thought to be given to the overall situation if you&apos;re going to complete your objectives. There&apos;s nothing quite like dogfighting in-between two MASSIVE capital ships, dodging the exchanging broadsides of beam cannon fire and flak bursts whilst trying to get a lock on your enemy. It can be a pretty heady experience.

It&apos;s a real shame that the genre has since died out (although may be on the verge of a resurgence given the number of space combat sims coming out of Russia at the moment). For the time being though, this decade old game, combined with the graphical updates from the Source Code Project, will more than see you through your desire for one more space combat game. A classic that&apos;s definitely stood the test of time.

Hit your burners pilot!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>37237806-93f9-11e8-a4f3-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Nothing like it since</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">99</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/375958da-30f1-11eb-b328-fa163eecd9d4 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/375958da-30f1-11eb-b328-fa163eecd9d4">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-11-27T22:43:33</review:date>
        <review:description>If you like the old type of cRPGs like Baldur&apos;s Gate or Neverwinter Nights, this is a game that worths give a try. Soon it will drag you into a pure western fantasy world. The setting. Specially the art, the music, gives everything a lover of western RPGs is looking for</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>375958da-30f1-11eb-b328-fa163eecd9d4</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Ideal game for the lovers of western RPG</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">17</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/379a9034-e9f0-11ec-8c07-42010a340c25 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/379a9034-e9f0-11ec-8c07-42010a340c25">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-06-12T04:37:27</review:date>
        <review:description>This game is great. I&apos;ve put in many hours &amp;amp; the story so far is good, it has twists and turns, it keeps me wanting to progress forward. However I&apos;d highly highly &amp;amp; I mean highly recommend going casual so you can quicksave in dungeons unless you&apos;re a real masochist or hardcore gamer b/c you&apos;ll get you __ handed to you on the regular in dungeons. That is a fact. I spent 4+ hours in one *area* of a single dungeon trying to take out an Orc king and in the end I got bodychecked into a cloud of poison smoke w/ nothing more to show for it for my troubles.

You can&apos;t change difficulty once you start so I&apos;m trying to save you a lot of wasted time, it&apos;s too late for me. I found a nice place above ground w/ some bears and moster spiders in new anthur bay to grind my days away hoping eventually to level up enough to brave some of the trickier dungeons. 

Another tip is make sure you have back up saves, relatively recent ones, if you accidentally leave or enter a dungeon with low health you can bet bottom dollar you&apos;ll meet respawned enemies all up in your business right at the entrance and good luck scrambling away before they kill you immediately. 

The world is huge I&apos;ve been playing for weeks and I doubt I&apos;ve seen 25% of the total map, and that&apos;s just above ground I&apos;m talking about. Keep a few scrolls of recall handy at all times b/c another thing you can bank on if you fashion yourself an explorer across the lands is you&apos;ll get tuned up real nice in a hurry by some new enemy that&apos;s 4 levels higher suddenly than anything on the previous map. 

All that said the game keeps me coming back because it&apos;s fun and the world is compelling. The writing even w/ your average NPC is quite good, in many ways it reminds me of Baldur&apos;s gate 1 &amp;amp; 2. I&apos;ve heard it compared to Diablo but to me this game has more heart and is not an arpg although there is plently of battling enemies. The graphics are nothing special but are serviceable &amp;amp; better than some suggest, w/ great variety of enemies. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>379a9034-e9f0-11ec-8c07-42010a340c25</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Engrossing but Punishing </review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3790ea70-2cbb-11e9-8f8a-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/3790ea70-2cbb-11e9-8f8a-00163e97122a">
