    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/458e0668-507a-11ec-92d9-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/458e0668-507a-11ec-92d9-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-11-28T20:37:42</review:date>
        <review:description>A game that drops you into the action without bothering to introduce the player character, the world, the conflict or the opponents. 

Half a level (?) later you&apos;re stuck with nowhere left to go. Yes, there&apos;s a door, with the nuclear symbol on it. That helps me about as much as writing &quot;door&quot; on it, given that there are no nukes to be found anywhere — just an empty level with a few respawning enemies. 

I thought playing MDK would erase the giant question mark that game has been in my mind since seeing it on the cover of some PC Gamer magazine, but nope — still as blurry as ever. Well, I&apos;m thoroughly bored after backtracking to the very beginning without spotting a single clue as to what to do next, and the handful of pixels that represent the environment aren&apos;t exactly beautiful enough to keep me running around aimlessly and endlessly. 


I don&apos;t see what&apos;s good about this, and since I don&apos;t seem to be allowed to progress any further, this is what I have to base my review on. I can&apos;t remember having played any other old game on here that has had trouble with these basic things. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>458e0668-507a-11ec-92d9-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>If you enjoy being stuck without clues.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


