    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5f8c5718-d029-11ea-925b-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5f8c5718-d029-11ea-925b-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-07-27T19:51:10</review:date>
        <review:description>The first 8 levels or so in the Varsilia in the game are amazing. After that the quests really taper off and it becomes a big grind. It feels like the game was abandoned in the 2nd half with significantly less interesting content.

Still worth it at the price for the first half. Just expect to not finish it (beyond west Varsilia+East Mercia).

Also the classes are not balanced. Rouge is worthless. Tank is ok but tanking is better done by companion (as they are immortal). The cleric is significantly more powerful than the rest (heals and capable nukes), with wizard being pretty decent as well.
</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">15</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>5f8c5718-d029-11ea-925b-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Fantastic first half but then drops off</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">51</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5f8ea3ba-c78b-11eb-82ce-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5f8ea3ba-c78b-11eb-82ce-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-06-07T15:24:58</review:date>
        <review:description>The initial tutorial indicates that the map will be revealed as you explore.  This ends up not being true.  When you dock at a port, the port is not added to your map.  As it turns out, the map is only updated when you purchase charts from a cartographer NPC.

It&apos;s puzzling how, after visiting a port, you wouldn&apos;t know where that port was.  This also prevents effective trading as you can&apos;t simply trade between known points you&apos;ve already discovered.

What would make sense would be to add ports to the map when you visit them.  Buying a chart from a cartographer would add areas you haven&apos;t yet visited to your map, and that would make sense.  But it&apos;s not logical to have visited a port and not then know where that is on your map.

As it is now, it discourages exploration and trade and doesn&apos;t seem like a logical design choice.

A possible workaround may be to screenshot the map screen when you dock at a port and manually add your own markers :)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>5f8ea3ba-c78b-11eb-82ce-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>map gameplay hurts exploration and trade</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">13</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5f9eeccc-ccfe-11e9-ac68-ea7091e34388 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5f9eeccc-ccfe-11e9-ac68-ea7091e34388">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-09-01T21:20:50</review:date>
        <review:description>If you are into Stardew Valley/Harvest Moon style games, you might really enjoy this game. Even though there is lots to do and it can feel overwhelming at first (don&apos;t let that get you down - use the online wikis if you need help crafting and assembling, they&apos;re really helpful!), you quickly find your routine and it gets very relaxing. The music and visuals are wonderful and make the game a great escape from the daily rut.

As for some negative bits that seem to be discussed in lots of reviews here on gog: My version of the game received a patch in early August 2019; I have not yet experienced any crash whatsoever; and the first-launch loading times were very long, but any loading times after that were acceptable. Not snappy, but acceptable.

I&apos;d recommend this game to anyone who is looking for a relaxing &amp;amp; visually pleasing open-world crafting &amp;amp; farming RPG. I&apos;d buy it again in a heartbeat.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>5f9eeccc-ccfe-11e9-ac68-ea7091e34388</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Beautiful &amp;amp; Relaxing</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5fe83b32-bca9-11ec-a676-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5fe83b32-bca9-11ec-a676-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-04-15T13:46:58</review:date>
        <review:description>The feeling of reaching milestones in this game brings a smile to my face like no other game. This game will make you think and will make you feel smart. The visuals and soundtrack are brilliant. I can say no bad thing about this game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>5fe83b32-bca9-11ec-a676-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>One of the greatest</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/50ad6b80-3f66-11ec-8222-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/50ad6b80-3f66-11ec-8222-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-11-07T03:02:01</review:date>
        <review:description>I would like to begin this review by saying that Black Mirror, The Dig, Broken Sword &amp;amp; Gabriel Knight are in the top 10 Point &amp;amp; Click games list of my personal preference. All 4 games have been around during the golden days of the Point &amp;amp; Click era and most of the genre lovers will agree with me that they are very highly regarded and respected.

There is an obvious pattern in my selection. I love a well-crafted serious mystery game with a twist of darkness and sometimes even a drop of supernatural. The eerie atmosphere of both Black Mirror &amp;amp; Gabriel Knight haunted me for weeks after completion. That means they did a thoroughly good job of getting under my skin and succumb me into their immersive atmosphere and story line. For me that is the difference between a good game and a genre defining game.

Saint Kotar was on my radar for quite sometime now, since it was the only tittle of recent years that ticked those boxes in my books. Sure, I enjoyed the Blackwell Series or Lamplight City but if I must be honest, they felt nothing like my personal favorite titles. Saint Kotar was promising to give me the dosage I craved for. And it delivered almost perfectly on that promise!

The guys at Red Martyr did a brilliant job at a time where the genre is not getting many games that are faithful to the “purebred” PNC games that we all love. And I am sure the tried to do exactly that. Stay true to the “cause” while stamping their own authority and signature in a “new age” PNC game. 

 </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>50ad6b80-3f66-11ec-8222-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Had me glued to the screend throughout!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/50f2c780-9151-11ea-9463-fa163e93294d -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/50f2c780-9151-11ea-9463-fa163e93294d">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-05-08T20:28:22</review:date>
        <review:description>Alright, so first of all: The game isn&apos;t subtle. But then why would it be, reality isn&apos;t very subtle either. It quite obviously pokes fun at capitalist and totalitarian themes by exaggerating them to cartoon-levels.
Most importantly, as the game proudly mentions, it is developed by a worker-owned studio, which is commendable in itself. And the theme of supporting workers rights shouldn&apos;t be controversial to any reasonable adult human beings, one would hope.

Anyway, when it comes down to it it&apos;s a straight-forward brawler with fun core gameplay (if &quot;fun&quot; is the right word considering the subject). And the soundtrack is quite banging as well, so there&apos;s that.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">229</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>50f2c780-9151-11ea-9463-fa163e93294d</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>As subtle as it needs to be</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">301</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/500e5166-943e-11e8-a083-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/500e5166-943e-11e8-a083-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2010-10-27T04:31:05</review:date>
        <review:description>This is one of the best single player FPS I ever played.
The art style is insanely atmospheric, the cut scenes are awesome, it&apos;s not too long or too short and it sure has one of the best looking flamethrowers ever made.

And honestly, I don&apos;t understand how some people can say it&apos;s a hard game, just lover the difficulty level if it&apos;s that hard for you - there&apos;s nothing saying that you need to complete games on hardest - all the time.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>500e5166-943e-11e8-a083-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>One of the best!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/504bccd8-93ff-11e8-aadb-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/504bccd8-93ff-11e8-aadb-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2009-09-10T08:42:10</review:date>
        <review:description>I love space games. From Elite (and Elite 2: Frontier) to Freelancer, and from Colony Wars to X3, I snap them up like candy.. and there have been far too few attempts at them that this is immediately an attractive game to me.

It&apos;s exactly what it says on the tin: A space combat sim. The controls are friendly, the combat is what you&apos;d expect, and the story is adequate... but I don&apos;t love it. 
The story isn&apos;t what you&apos;d get in Wing Commander, and in those you get a similar combat experience too. It reminds me of a less-polished Colony Wars, in fact.. or a hybrid of the two... but perhaps I was weaned and different games, and Freespacers would think the reverse. :)
I wasn&apos;t, though, and this is my review.
It fights to a four because damn it, it&apos;s got pep.. and even if it isn&apos;t quite up to either of those in my eyes, it doesn&apos;t deserve a three!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>504bccd8-93ff-11e8-aadb-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Not quite there for me...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5040b142-e304-11ec-a341-42010a340c25 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5040b142-e304-11ec-a341-42010a340c25">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-06-03T09:13:40</review:date>
        <review:description>Are you bored and horny? Looking for a way to kill an hour? Need a pick-me-up game to start the night? Well then look no further because this game is perfect for the task! I&apos;ll be honest with you, this game is barely a game, mechanically speaking. The only interaction you have while engaging in the main game mode is &apos;aim&apos; and &apos;shoot&apos;. That said, what this game lacks in depth it makes up for in EXPLOSIONS VIOLENCE and JIGGLE PHYSICS.
The way the game works is that there are 6 colored balls (enemies) and 1 white ball (you) on a field with obstacles, rabbit men, and bunny girls. The goal is to hit the enemy balls with your ball to create explosions that rocket the balls into other balls making more explosions, and so on and so forth. These projectiles then impact the rabbit men (including yourself so be careful) knocking them out, which gives you free reign to run around the map &quot;collecting&quot; the bunny girls the only way rabbits know how. Each round has a timer to limit how much &quot;collecting&quot; you can do per round, which is extended whenever a knockout occurs. But be careful, because any rabbit men that are still standing are also free to &quot;collect&quot; the bunnies they run in to. If any two rabbits cross paths theyll square up and slug it out. When you &quot;collect&quot; all 6 bunnies in a stage, you win. Easy peasy!
So yeah, not a deep game, but if you&apos;re in a gaming rut, try this one out to get you back up and ready for action. Also, did I mention that everybody is constantly dancing to smooth jazz? No? Well they do! Its actually a very nicely animated game for something so... indie, let&apos;s say.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>5040b142-e304-11ec-a341-42010a340c25</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Rabbiculous and ballsy</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/505b7468-109d-11eb-8f43-fa163edf6d96 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/505b7468-109d-11eb-8f43-fa163edf6d96">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-10-17T20:22:20</review:date>
        <review:description>It&apos;s hard to describe what a wonderful experience this game is. It&apos;s unique but intuitive, cerebral but not convoluted, rich and yet streamlined and contained, with an interesting story that keeps you at a slight distance all the time, making you constantly engaged.

And all this made by one guy.

This is just such a great indie development accomplishment, that i&apos;d encourage everyone to try it out. Get in knowing as little as possible - you won&apos;t be disappointed.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>505b7468-109d-11eb-8f43-fa163edf6d96</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Amazing experience</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


