    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/514bc6b6-c1b1-11e9-8211-02d552e6ec0c -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/514bc6b6-c1b1-11e9-8211-02d552e6ec0c">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-08-18T12:11:32</review:date>
        <review:description>Pathologic 1 was a unique experience in more than one way. I dare say that developers calling it life-changing isn&apos;t far from truth. It was a unique story experienced in a unique way. And, well, a pretty bad game. There is no point in discussing it here - most would agree that it was only a unique vision that kept the first game together.

This one keeps the vision while learning from all the mistakes that the first game has made. It also manages to look amazing - contrary to the first one that looked bad even on release. It does a good job in delivering an actual game that is good to play while not losing the narrative part.

One warning, though. It&apos;s a hard game, but it&apos;s not hard in a way that you are meant to get good at it or else you&apos;ll struggle. Quite the opposite: you are meant to struggle and that is going to deliver you the best experience possible. Keep going, keep failing and those failures are going to be the best failures of your life.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>514bc6b6-c1b1-11e9-8211-02d552e6ec0c</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>An artistic vision made into a good game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/515dc4a6-f8e2-11ea-9ecf-fa163e7ab9c9 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/515dc4a6-f8e2-11ea-9ecf-fa163e7ab9c9">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-09-17T15:35:49</review:date>
        <review:description>这个游戏的特色是随着背景音乐的播放，会有光标不断生成并靠近准星（类似音游），你需要掌握适当节奏才能使用射击，换弹和技能，三者的特定音效搭配音乐，使得游戏充满了节奏感。我没接触过音游，在进行了2个多小时的游玩后，才掌握节奏。
我玩了好一会才知道能连跳，“空中连跳”加“冲刺”，对躲避敌方攻击和到达隐藏地点很有帮助。游戏角色能装备四个装备（头，手，足，盾），不同组合能带来不同效果。有一些imba组合，例如无限子弹（盾），伤害回血（头）和致命伤害（足），武器配机关枪（不需要掌握节奏的枪，一直按着）。我使用了上面这套组合（机关枪是第一关宝箱开的），游戏难度大大降低，通过了最后一关。在通关过程中还用普通钥匙兑换了“骷髅钥匙”（骷髅=无穷普通），游戏难度再次降低。
游戏很不错，但环境的色调过于单一，呈现为红色（天使屠杀红色地狱），部分玩家可能感觉不适。一次只能装备一把武器，相比DOOM的可切换武器缺了些乐趣，但是随机的地牢和战利品，以及不同的角色，使得游戏可玩性还算不错。
推荐观看Youtube上的”8 Minutes of BPM: Bullets Per Minute Gameplay“，再选择是否购买。
</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>515dc4a6-f8e2-11ea-9ecf-fa163e7ab9c9</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>节奏FPS</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


