    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5574fdaa-6fc7-11eb-b20a-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/5574fdaa-6fc7-11eb-b20a-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-02-15T21:52:28</review:date>
        <review:description>El juego está bueno, lindos graficos, buena mecánica de juego. 
Pero hecho en falta cosas que en estos días traen todos los juegos, por ejemplo: no se puede cambiar la configuración de los comandos. Esto es básico, la configuración que trae por defecto no me queda para nada cómoda. 
Por otro lado dentro de cada capítulo vas pasando por puntos de control, pero, si salis del juego cuando volves a jugar arrancas del principio del mismo, no importa si estabas casi terminandolo, tenes que hacer todo de nuevo. Esto, para mi, es desmotivante.
</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>5574fdaa-6fc7-11eb-b20a-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Bueno, con detalles...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/558f4332-0752-11eb-99cf-fa163e7ab9c9 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/558f4332-0752-11eb-99cf-fa163e7ab9c9">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-10-06T00:32:56</review:date>
        <review:description>This game has a great atmosphere and the story isn&apos;t that boring, but:
- it&apos;s very short. It took me a couple hours of gameplay time to finish it.
- it&apos;s repetitive. All you do is basically collect highlighted stuff, and use it on other highlighted stuff. No riddles, no puzzles, just grab something (a key for example), take it to some other place and use it to advance (like open a door).
- There are just a couple of enemies in the entire game, and both are similar, and you just hide to avoid them. Nothing spectacular or challenging.

So overall, it&apos;s a simple one-time play, not something I will be returning to, nor will I be recommending it.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>558f4332-0752-11eb-99cf-fa163e7ab9c9</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>It&apos;s nice, but..</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/55803d6c-52b8-11ec-8157-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/55803d6c-52b8-11ec-8157-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-12-01T17:07:00</review:date>
        <review:description>Ok, look... I see the screenshots the same as you. I know by today&apos;s standards, this is kinda janky. However, I played this game in middle school and have such a fondness for it, I had to leap on reviewing it. 

The story is inventive and the setting is really interesting. The puzzles are generally in a sweet spot of making sense without devolving into the gratuitous moon logic some adventure games spiraled into during that genre&apos;s downfall in the 90s (with an exception that&apos;s explicitly on purpose and is signposted in-game due to that pocket universe being explicitly nonsensical. I promise it makes more sense in-game, but it might be the more annoying world. Think Alice in Wonderland or Animaniacs levels of &quot;physics doesn&apos;t exactly work here, just go with it&quot;). 
I&apos;m getting ahead of myself. The general premise is that there are those in the distant future who can create &quot;pocket universes&quot;. These function as a sort of parallel universe with a setting, occupants, and even rules of physics that are all designed by their creator. They can be very earthlike or very... not. Several of these have been thrown into stasis, freezing their entire universe and their inhabitants in time. You need to enter them and un-freeze them by interacting with each universe&apos;s McGuffin that acts as it&apos;s main control center. 

In addition to the standard point-and-click affair of picking up random stuff and combining it or applying it to the world in some way, you also have your multitool that is shaped by the physics of the world. However, the marquee is Bottled Time. It&apos;s what allows you to move freely in a universe stuck in stasis (as well as anyone/thing you stand directly beside), but it also allows you to unfreeze items at a distance if you toss a ball of the stuff at the object. This leads to some interesting puzzle solving opportunities. Just don&apos;t run out... or walk under a falling rock.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>55803d6c-52b8-11ec-8157-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Gem from my youth</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">41</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/56ab12b8-3434-11ec-9775-fa163e99870e -->

