        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Pairs well with soy milk.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">54</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73dc3e70-41f7-11e9-a7a6-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73dc3e70-41f7-11e9-a7a6-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-03-09T01:11:06</review:date>
        <review:description>I have played through this game to completion when it first launched on PC. I remember picking this game up for $10 back then in jewel case and still have my physical copy even today. At the time I was quite taken with the game. Unfortunately as time went on? The game like many others became a bit of a chore to get running on modern machines and you&apos;d have to download a few files from some dedicated modders. These updated launchers often required you to have the original disc. But what do you do if your disc has become unreadable over time? It can unfortunately happen which makes digital preservation and re-releases of these old games so important. 

Indiana Jones and the Infernal Machine is a 3D platformer/adventure game. The game is set during the cold war and it&apos;s up to you to unearth the secrets of the fabled infernal machine before the soviets do. It&apos;s a surprisingly engaging Indiana Jones tale aided by some well voiced dialogue. Unlike the N64 version? The PC version has models where the mouths of the character models are animated. 

The controls do feel a bit dated compared to more modern games. They will take some getting used to as you control Indy with WASD. Many of the actions in the game are context sensitive so if you&apos;ve played other platformers you will probably pick it up pretty easily. 

The levels are varied in their locales, if a bit linear. The puzzles are clever but not too challenging. Treasure can be found which allows you to purchase ammo, med kits, anti venom, etc. between levels. There are even a few driving stages

The animations are pretty well done considering this is a title that came out in 1999. Ocassionally a guard will dodge by rolling to the side. 

On GOG Galaxy? The game gave me a problem where it would crash at launch. Upon browsing the forums here? Some pointed to Headsets causing problems. I unplugged my wireless USB headset dongle it launched.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>73dc3e70-41f7-11e9-a7a6-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Crack that whip, It&apos;s classic Indy!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73e0a5e8-93f9-11e8-869d-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73e0a5e8-93f9-11e8-869d-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2008-10-24T12:53:06</review:date>
        <review:description>I agree with the negatives that the game got stuck in a rut at times, but I enjoyed the world and storytelling (and personality) a lot that i didnt mind trying a fight many times or turning in circles at times. There&apos;s not enough steampunk in games to pass this one :)</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>73e0a5e8-93f9-11e8-869d-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>better than what most reviews said</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73e62ab8-a4da-11ec-8248-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73e62ab8-a4da-11ec-8248-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-03-16T05:37:49</review:date>
        <review:description>I want to like Blackwind. Perhaps simply by the fact that there&apos;s not enough top-down action mech games,  perhaps because there seems to be sincere effort put into it. But no matter how hard I want to like it, I can&apos;t.

The game is very barebones, tries to do many things but does absolutely nothing in a remarkable or even above-average way, it has many issues, flaws and game-breaking bugs, and the general lack of polish, testing, etc., is incredibly apparent. Beyond that, the underlying ideas and systems for the game, which can&apos;t be easily addressed with post-release bugfixes and patches, are generally weak and underdeveloped or too safe/cliche - the story, for example, is cookie-cutter and mind-numbing, to the point where I had a podcast on in the background and started to not bother pausing it during cutscenes.

Gameplay seems alright at first, if a bit anemic and brainless, but as it goes on and on it simply becomes too repetitive and shallow, and the game&apos;s attempts at spicing it up by introducing new enemies or mechanics don&apos;t do enough to freshen things up, nor make combat any more challenging. Other than stomping aliens and such, the game consists of trudging along backtracking through the same areas several times or doing some janky puzzle platforming - again, the game&apos;s trying to do a lot of things but doesn&apos;t do any well enough.

Visually, the game feels like someone slapped a generic sci-fi asset pack down into UE4&apos;s default rendering pipeline and didn&apos;t have time to do anything beyond just implementing it and building the levels: beyond a handful of models and textures, everything looks generic, ready-made and devoid of its own style, atmosphere, mood, anything visually distinguishing. The camera also feels like a premade thing, or something very poorly slapped together through blueprints to &apos;get the job done&apos; and nothing else. There&apos;s stuff that shows effort, like the executions, but even that is marred by poor implementation and becomes repetitive.
</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>73e62ab8-a4da-11ec-8248-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Not completely irredeemable, but bad</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73f2d688-9448-11e8-b4f9-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/73f2d688-9448-11e8-b4f9-00163e97122a">
