    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75d15f8e-5389-11ec-8651-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75d15f8e-5389-11ec-8651-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-12-02T18:03:59</review:date>
        <review:description>I love that kind of games, easy in a good way, without real dangers, stress-free, just something to experience in a beautiful world. The only thing out of that are some uncomfortable moments in the last chapter, but it&apos;s part of the good things at the same time, and overall very satisfying to play.
The things that are not told but you can glimpse if you&apos;re a little sensitive add a touch of depth to the story.
I don&apos;t usually have the feeling of wanting to play a game again and with this one I could because I feel it wouldn&apos;t be less satisfying the second time.

Apart from that it&apos;s also a good thing that the game is saved at any time just when you exit. The next time you&apos;ll be in the exact spot with the same achievements done. It should be a feature of all games in my opinion. It&apos;s annoying having to repeat something or not being able to stop when you want or need. So very happy with that.

And a strange thing is you also have access to every chapter from the beginning. I appreciate the lack of restrinctions.

If you need difficult challenges to feel fulfilled I don&apos;t recommend it to you, but this game is not about that and they have managed not to make it boring by any means. I love it!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>75d15f8e-5389-11ec-8651-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Calming and satisfying</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75f12d9a-5287-11eb-a6db-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75f12d9a-5287-11eb-a6db-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-01-09T16:32:11</review:date>
        <review:description>It&apos;s a pretty alright 3D Platformer with passable melee combat and a lot of rough edges.

I enjoyed my first couple of hours with the game quite a lot. I love the art style and general design of the world, but you do start to see where they had to cut corners pretty fast.
The combat gets really repetitive really quickly and never adds anything to make it more complex or enjoyable. Yes, you do pick up a new weapon every now and again, but the never fundamentally change anything about how you are going to approach a given situation. Enemy variety is also pretty lacking.

Furthermore, the levels never up the gameplay in any big ways. Sure, you have gimmicks that they then stretch out over entire levels, so even these sections get boring in the levels that they are introduced in.

That pretty much is the entire game for me...neat little gimmicks that never really build on a solid foundation, but instead add a little bit too it that then fizzles out over a couple of minutes after it has been introduced.

I would like to see them make a fleshed out sequel or spiritual successor that improves on this foundation. 
The game had quite a bit of potential, but never does anything remarkable besides looking very pretty.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>75f12d9a-5287-11eb-a6db-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Neat, but not that neat</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75fe9a70-d88e-11ea-8256-fa163eb9e0ef -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75fe9a70-d88e-11ea-8256-fa163eb9e0ef">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-08-07T12:14:56</review:date>
        <review:description>Only at life&apos;s end, you see that your whole life was a lesson, and you were a careless student at it. 
There I stand with my lesson unlearned. The teacher is out. &quot;Pack your books and leave&quot;. I would be glad to be &quot;punished&quot; by someone, to be &quot;left without lunch&quot;. But no one will punish. No one needs you. Tomorrow, there will be a &quot;lesson&quot;. But for others. And others will study. You will never be taught again.
(V. V. Rozanov, &quot;Fallen Leaves&quot;, 1913, and my best effort to translate it to English)

In Pathologic 2 you play the role of Haruspex who left his hometown to study medicine. He receives a worrying letter from his father and takes the train home to find his father dead, his friends changed dramatically, and everyone thinking he has killed his father. A few in-game days later, things go bad.

The game is unfair. It is unfair to the point that you have a dialog option to say how unfair it is. There is always a chance that you loose even if you do everything right. You can spend your day running around the town protecting your friends to find out that Fortuna turned away from them and some of them are dead. Just like in a real life.

The game is hard. Survival mechanisms may seem annoying, but they are important for the experience. At some point, you&apos;ll find yourself starving to death. There is barter economy in the Town, and you approach a child to offer him/her marbles or a bug in a box, but you don&apos;t have enough. Still... you know, you can kill the child to get food. It is not about &quot;possible or not&quot;, it is about moral choices. Just like in real life.

The game is fascinating. Striving to feed Haruspex and change (or at least see) the fate of the Town, I was constantly forgetting to eat.

The game is cruel. Surprisingly, not as cruel as real life: you will be punished and &quot;left without lunch&quot; in it. 

The game is all about death.

This game is definitely worth playing.

P.S. For those who want the citation in Russian, search for &quot;вся твоя жизнь была поучением&quot;.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>75fe9a70-d88e-11ea-8256-fa163eb9e0ef</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Sister Death</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75422c5e-ca4a-11e8-aabd-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/75422c5e-ca4a-11e8-aabd-00163e97122a">
