    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/802e383e-c3f5-11e8-ace3-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/802e383e-c3f5-11e8-ace3-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-09-27T05:38:45</review:date>
        <review:description>This game has the same problems as part 1, namely it is a non-interactive story (or barely-interactive). You are on rails and eventually you end up following every update and just putting datachunks at random to advance the plot.
One change is that you now have a clock, and actions take time. This does not mean that it is relevant. The advisor (which is really annoying) makes you think that this is the case, but I&apos;ve missed some deadlines and nothing serious happened.
Another change is that you have some input boxes as well. That makes you think a little bit, in the sense that you have to choose a datachunk from a profile to upload. Needed to advance the plot, but you won&apos;t lose sleep trying to figure out what to input.
As for the rest, just read my review of Orwell while you find something else to play (like Floor 13).</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>802e383e-c3f5-11e8-ace3-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Flawed execution, like part 1</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/8050d1b4-8e82-11eb-b844-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/8050d1b4-8e82-11eb-b844-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-03-27T00:27:51</review:date>
        <review:description>I know price is not to be mentioned in reviews, but this is a product, products have prices, and value of purchase can sometimes only be fairly assessed that way. Worth at most $4. If undead, witchhunter, globadier, and mercenary were all just released as one damn expansion instead of a half dozen dlc&apos;s for maybe $12 combined, okay. This is just awful. If it was a full game or expansion, sure, I could see leaving on price in the review. This is pain in the ass cash grab overpriced micro transaction crap, and we should be allowed to call a spade a spade. This is not a game. It is not an expansion to a game really. It is a product sold within a game and it should be considered fair to rate and judge it as such.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>8050d1b4-8e82-11eb-b844-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Keep your TEN dollars.</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">28</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/81bd405e-a9c7-11eb-8d39-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/81bd405e-a9c7-11eb-8d39-fa163e99870e">
