    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/82de7436-4071-11eb-8bd3-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/82de7436-4071-11eb-8bd3-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-12-17T16:09:43</review:date>
        <review:description>En voyant des vidéos de Slipstream, j&apos;ai eu un coup de foudre: ça m&apos;a immédiatement rappelé mes sessions sur Outrun lors de ma jeunesse. Et visuellement, le jeu tient ses promesses et l&apos;ambiance est là.

MAIS le gros problème du jeu, c&apos;est sa mécanique de drift lors des virages, qui donne l&apos;impression de fonctionner 1 fois sur 5 (et encore, je suis généreux) et comme pour chaque virage à grande vitesse, le drift est indispensable, on se prend le décor dans la tronche malgré notre bonne volonté.

Second problème du drift: comment l&apos;activer; il faut relâcher la touche RT de la manette, puis appuyer brièvement sur LT et ensuite represser RT aussi sec. C&apos;est complètement pas intuitif, et résulte qu&apos;à chaque virage on lutte pour faire son foutu drift et soit on ralenti énormément, soit en dévie hors du chemin. Très frustrant.

Dernier problème rageant: on ne peut PAS régler les touches de la manette (alors que le clavier, oui), et donc on se retrouve puni d&apos;essayer d&apos;y jouer à la manette. Super.

Messieurs les devs, faites tester votre jeu avant de le vendre, et par des personnes autres que des amis complaisants parce que là le plaisir de jeu est vraiment gâcher pour ces choix de game design absolument mauvais.

Nota Bene: on peut bien mettre le jeu en drift automatique, mais on perd en vitesse bien plus qu&apos;en mode manuel infernal.

Je mets 3 gentilles étoiles pour le bel emballage rétro très réussi tout de même.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>82de7436-4071-11eb-8bd3-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>[FR] Maniabilité calamiteuse...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/82ecd24e-815d-11e9-9d0c-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/82ecd24e-815d-11e9-9d0c-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-05-28T18:30:24</review:date>
        <review:description>While the game might look nice and gog has made a fantastic job in making in run on modern computers, It&apos;s just unfortunately what might have been revolutionary back in 1997 just feel so boring nowadays.

There is just no depth to this game, just run around and  shoot everything on the screen until they die with little to no motivation. As the game just dumps you into a level with no cut scene or anything to entice you to play it.

Get it if you enjoy mindless shooters. Otherwise play something else.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>82ecd24e-815d-11e9-9d0c-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>A mindless shooter</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/82fb808c-832e-11e9-81cb-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/82fb808c-832e-11e9-81cb-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-05-31T01:59:00</review:date>
        <review:description>Get Darkest Dungeon, remove everything that made it a great game, increase the difficulty (through VERY BAD design choices): This is Vambrace: Cold Soul.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">22</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>82fb808c-832e-11e9-81cb-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>Darkest Dungeon clone? sadly no</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">12</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/821459bc-3e63-11ec-a4fe-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/821459bc-3e63-11ec-a4fe-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-11-05T20:09:24</review:date>
        <review:description>Adventure games are supposed to have puzzles, whereas in this game they are basically non-existent. The last puzzle is an exception and a real face-slapper, since most actions before that are trivial.

The crowdfunding demo showed a lot of promise. I&apos;m sad to say that mostly, I feel, it was underdelivered. In comparison, the demo featured a lot more objects to investigate and interact with, far more mysterious and eerie prologue and at least three or four different paths which you could take to complete it. I was also looking forward to some answers for the mystery behind Silent Hill-ish radio from the souvenir shop. Nothing about it in the final game, though.

Keeping in mind that this is a low budget project and a very first from the Red Martyr, it does end up being a pretty solid product overall. The lack of puzzles is compensated by some rather outstanding writing and dialogues. Be prepared for a lot of reading, and a lot of walking. Double-clicking makes the character run so this speeds up travel a bit, and in some cases there&apos;s also a handy dialog option to get to a certain destination immediately. For better orientation there is also a non-interactive map of town with surroundings, and a &apos;task list&apos; with active/completed sections. The art is compelling and fits the setting nicely. All of the text is enriched with voice acting, which generally does not disappoint, especially in the case of playable characters. You can tell when the actors put their souls into the role. The music isn&apos;t very varied, but there were a couple tunes that got stuck in my head long after I was done playing. This is all a credit to the overall atmosphere and mood. 

Lastly, a tip - keep at least one save game from previous area as a backup. I made a mistake not to and got stuck almost at end. D:

As a backer I really, really wanted to give this a better score, but the things that irked me were just too prominent. Great potential this team has, and I&apos;m looking forward to more from them.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>821459bc-3e63-11ec-a4fe-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>A solid interactive story</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">15</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/823c4dc4-949c-11e8-a867-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/823c4dc4-949c-11e8-a867-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2012-01-24T18:08:43</review:date>
        <review:description>Bought this game when it first came out, having been a huge fan of both Descent and FreeSpace 1 before it.

Found my original disks in the loft the other day - had no luck installing them on Windows 7 64-bit - but had no problem installing via the version hosted here on GOG.

The game runs fine as things stand - but if you want to see this in high resolutions and with modern eye-candy such as &apos;shine maps&apos; and much more detailed nebulas, ships and asteroids take a look at the FreeSpace Open project.

My old Logitech Wingman joystick has come out of a 10 year retirement and I&apos;m loving working my way back through the classic campaign mode.

If you ever played the X-Wing or Privateer series - this is the game that took that genre to another level.

Highly recommended pure dog-fighting joy!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>823c4dc4-949c-11e8-a867-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A classic that runs great on Win 7 x64</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


