    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/85c2e908-32e9-11e9-ae49-00163e4e60cc -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/85c2e908-32e9-11e9-ae49-00163e4e60cc">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-02-17T21:23:36</review:date>
        <review:description>Note: the game is unplayable until you&apos;ve learned about the &quot;drift&quot; and &quot;slipstream&quot; mechanics, so make sure to play the tutorial. You can thank me later.

Overall, the game has a nice retro arcade feel to it that might make you nostalgic if you played arcades as a kid in the 80s (and probably 90s too). The execution is good, the game is stable, the atmosphere and music are spot on.

So far, I haven&apos;t found the game to have incredible amounts of depth so I don&apos;t see anyone playing this for hours on end, but if you have a 15-30 minutes break in your day and need to play something quick, it can be a relaxing de-stressor. The races are not that long.

Not sure how much appeal the game has for someone who wasn&apos;t around to play arcades in the 80s and early 90s. Unlike many other retro-themed indie games, I don&apos;t think this one adds much to the source material. I can&apos;t help for feel that they could have capitalized on combining the unique feels of old arcade racing games with a modern perspective to add something unique to the table that could have drawn younger gamers in, but they missed the mark there.

So overall, this is a nice recollection, but nothing revolutionary and probably a disappointment for gamers that don&apos;t have rose-tinted retro goggles to approach the game with.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>85c2e908-32e9-11e9-ae49-00163e4e60cc</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Not Bad, Not Great</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/85f5cc8e-5e02-11ea-87f0-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/85f5cc8e-5e02-11ea-87f0-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-03-04T12:25:51</review:date>
        <review:description>TLDR; BUY. THIS. GAME.

I&apos;m one the Kickstarter backers from 2013. I&apos;ve been playing since first public alpha release (ver. 0.1 IIRC). Cannot recommend this game enough. Replayability and longevity are out of this world. Estimated 500 hours clocked in, and I haven&apos;t even tried mods (or Royalty patch) yet.

I&apos;m a big fan of emergent storytelling (ie. stories that are created out of circumstances of the game, not by deliberate design), and RimWorld delivers in spades. Try to build the best base you can, or set yourself up for a glorious defeat with single minded ***holes and pensioners. Which ever way you choose to play, it&apos;ll be fun to watch these tortured creatures trying to make a life for themselves in an unforgiving world (or a lot more forgiving, depending on your settings).

I would recommend you to buy this game.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>85f5cc8e-5e02-11ea-87f0-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Emergent storytelling at its finest</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">16</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


