    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9d9bc2da-397a-11ec-8789-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9d9bc2da-397a-11ec-8789-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-10-30T15:12:13</review:date>
        <review:description>Take the golden oldie &apos;Mercenaries Playground of Destruction&apos; for PS2 or Xbox and swap out North Korea for a fictional Latin American tropical island. Well, that&apos;s the gist of 2006&apos;s Just Cause. Huge open world, jack vehicles, various mission types, lotsa side missions...you get the picture. Much like GTA3 for PC, Just Cause also has rock bottom hardware requirements so it&apos;ll run on anything from the past 15 years. Guaranteed to scratch that GTA or MPOD itch!

The only flaw here is no multiplayer, but the single player is still a blast regardless. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9d9bc2da-397a-11ec-8789-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Mercenaries PoD in Latin America!</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">11</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9da0c076-f914-11e9-893b-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9da0c076-f914-11e9-893b-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-10-28T01:50:55</review:date>
        <review:description>-- Since GOG won&apos;t accept more the 2000 characters, you can check my full review on my blog if you want. 

https://analisedasobras.blogspot.com/2019/10/slipstream-english-review.html

Here is a brief summary --

Despite its retro style, this is one of the most fun modern racing games I&apos;ve ever played. It is one of those that are born classic. It was heavily inspired by Outrun, but it doesn&apos;t feel like you are playing Outrun or a direct sequel. It feels more like a game made in the 90&apos;s for Sega CD or one of those classics EA games made for Mega Drive.

Both gameplay and artistic&apos;s choises feels like reminiscent of a bunch of games made by Sega, or at least made for Sega&apos;s consoles. I could feel also elements borrowed from Road Rash, Crazy Taxi, California Games and the list goes on. I think that the driftings should have reminded me NFS: Underground or any other game, but it actually reminded me Speed ​​Racer a lot (both the Wachoswki siblins film and its game adaptation), some tracks you just run sideways. It is like this: imagine if the Hollywood phisics were right. And as I love both Speed Racer film and game I was glad to feel this feeling. I also felt some nostalgic feeling of playing Atari&apos;s Enduro, I don&apos;t know why, pure nostalgia.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9da0c076-f914-11e9-893b-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>A classic born game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9dae66fa-4871-11ec-be11-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9dae66fa-4871-11ec-be11-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2021-11-18T15:15:35</review:date>
        <review:description>You can definitely make a case for Paradox putting more time and care into Stellaris DLCs early on. Leviathans goes a long way toward making the galaxy feel more alive. It is also a good DLC for beginners that struggle to obtain some of the later-game technologies. Not quite as essential as utopia, but do pick it up.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9dae66fa-4871-11ec-be11-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>More essential DLC</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


