        <review:title>Freespace Forever</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9e90b2fa-1b45-11e9-91ac-00163e4e60cc -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9e90b2fa-1b45-11e9-91ac-00163e4e60cc">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2019-01-18T19:22:23</review:date>
        <review:description>Lovely little game if you don&apos;t mind a bit of grind. What completely ruins it however, is lack of sounds like wind, weather, water, animals, etc. Turn down the repetitive music and you suddenly have a completely dead world... or a silent film from year 1895. Very poor design choice there.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">66</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9e90b2fa-1b45-11e9-91ac-00163e4e60cc</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Lacks some crucial features and polish</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">122</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9e913a2a-d6c9-11ec-a445-fa163e99870e -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9e913a2a-d6c9-11ec-a445-fa163e99870e">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-05-18T19:43:17</review:date>
        <review:description>I was originally interested in SOC just for the pixel art, and it turns out there’s a really deep game here, too! It took me to about mission 3 for some of the strategy elements to click (you won’t beat mission 3 if they don’t!) and wow it made beating the big bads feel like a real accomplishment! 

I’ve seen others compare it to Heroes of Might and Magic, which I haven’t played—to me it is a cross between Medieval II: Total War and Final Fantasy Tactics.

The writing is strong, but can use some polishing as is expected with early access games. I have encountered a few bugs but nothing game-breaking. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9e913a2a-d6c9-11ec-a445-fa163e99870e</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Come for the pixels, stay for the combat</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9eb0965e-c584-11e8-a391-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9eb0965e-c584-11e8-a391-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2012-11-25T19:00:33</review:date>
        <review:description>... but this game truly stands out. Damn is this game good.

It takes itself pretty seriously but, unlike other games who attempt the same thing, this one actually succeeds at it by being both believable and engaging!

You aren&apos;t held by the hand, like *many* modern games to date, but rather &quot;trained&quot; at becoming a successful space dogfighter. The cool thing is that nothing feels like a chore.


During a mission you&apos;ll get to:
+ escort ships
+ analyze cargo
+ disarm freighters 20 times your size
+ destroy many alien enemy squads
+ lock on and evade missiles (the former with the use of decoys called &quot;counter-measures&quot;)
+ issue commands to your squads in real time

and more!


Does it sound complex? Well, yeah, it is a little but it&apos;s definitely manageable! Trust me. As I said in the title, I&apos;m no sim fanatic: I usually find simulators to be a grind (requiring hours upon hours of studying before you can actually get accustomed to the game), but not this game. I picked it up, didn&apos;t read the manual for it, followed the 3 basic tutorial missions and I was ready to go. The missions felt balanced enough to make me learn more along the way, but that&apos;s not all.


The story and cutscenes are well constructed, with an exceptional voice acting (in my humble opinion), and you actually feel curious to discover what&apos;s going to happen next. 


Admitedly the plot isn&apos;t all that fresh, with a sort of Starcraft vibe to it, but by no means should this make the game feel unoriginal. I&apos;m probably past half-way of the story (I still haven&apos;t touched the expansion yet) and I&apos;m loving every part of it; with ambushes and surprises around every corner. I&apos;m legitimately hooked.


If that&apos;s not enough to keep you glued on the screen then the medals sure will. For each mission that you complete you receive a briefing with either a commendation for your success or reprimand for your failures; making every action feel like it has an impact! This shouldn&apos;t discourage you however because, if you fail in any way, the game comes to the rescue by giving you some tips to complete the game successfully by clicking on the &quot;recommendations&quot; button (IIRC). 

This way you get the feeling of being in command of a genuine mission without being constantly spoon fed while playing. How refreshing! 

Not only that, but if you manage to complete the bonus missions you receive various medals which, oddly enough for me, &quot;feel&quot; like you deserved them. 

=========
Conclusion
=========

This game is awesome and should be played at all costs.

I have played it with a keyboard and found it not bad but, let me tell you... I decided to give it a go with an XBox360 compatible controller (more precisely the Thrusmaster GP XID) and...

it... is... AWESOME!

I&apos;ve managed to map almost every single keyboard command on the controller by using Xpadder (except for the squad communication commands and energy/shield controls, which are easy to spot on the keyboard) and the level of immersion is fantastic. I managed to rake in 16 kills with no sweat and it feels great. It&apos;s been a while since the last time I&apos;ve felt this way for a game so, for the last time, I urge you to:

BUY THIS GAME! IT IS WORTH EVERY PENNY!</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9eb0965e-c584-11e8-a391-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>I&apos;m no space sim fanatic...</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9ee8d3c2-dd8d-11e8-9669-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9ee8d3c2-dd8d-11e8-9669-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2018-11-01T06:21:36</review:date>
        <review:description>This is a gorgeously detailed detective story of sailors, monsters and curses, created by Lucas Pope, whose previous work was the brilliant &quot;Papers, Please&quot;.

Set in the early 19th Century, and rendered in monochrome graphics designed to evoke the feel of (specifically) early Apple computers, the game wrings gorgous art out of self-imposed limitation.  Each pixel seems filled with ominous meaning.

You play an insurance assessor tasked with determining the fate of the 60 crew members missing from the ghost ship Obra Dinn, and you are armed with a magical stopwatch capable of replaying the moment of a person&apos;s death.  Each memory is a glorious 3D frozen tableau, every element clearly caught in the middle of dynamic motion, each actor&apos;s face full of emotion, suggesting a story that spins out in a dozen directions that beg to be followed.

You work from nothing more than a list that shows the names of crewmembers paired with their nationality and role on the ship, plus a series of hand-drawn artist impressions of the ship that capture the faces of the crew. It&apos;s up to you to match faces with names, and then determine the fate of each individual along with who (if anyone) is responsible. In accomplishing this, sometimes you&apos;re lucky enough to hear people named by other crewmembers, but more often you&apos;re working from accents, affiliations, uniforms, hammock positioning, distinguishing tattoos, or eventually just a process of elimination.

There&apos;s no hand-holding - the game is a giant crossword for you to solve in whatever order suits you, picking at the problems that intrigue you and deferring thorny ones for later. Even at the end, while the broad strokes of the Obra Dinn&apos;s tragedy become clear, the sea keeps for itself some of the darker secrets.

The whole thing is short enough to finish in a single afternoon, but the experience will stay with you long afterwards.  Five stars.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9ee8d3c2-dd8d-11e8-9669-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>The Finest Indie Game of the Year</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9f4f56be-feba-11eb-b4fa-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9f4f56be-feba-11eb-b4fa-fa163e9aa6fa">
