    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/97644bd6-9459-11e8-9bbb-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/97644bd6-9459-11e8-9bbb-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2012-04-24T11:36:03</review:date>
        <review:description>Remember playing this as a kid over and over again, didn&apos;t take long for me to relive that feeling after i downloaded it, great fun game but so immersive, the scenarios really put you in the middle of the action, well worth a go, even better if you have a joystick.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>97644bd6-9459-11e8-9bbb-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</review:rating>
        <review:title>Simply Amazing</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9794c738-81e6-11ec-b27c-fa163e9aa6fa -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/9794c738-81e6-11ec-b27c-fa163e9aa6fa">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2022-01-30T18:06:32</review:date>
        <review:description>Well, the first thing to note is that this is strictly a puzzle game. There are no sim/management elements whatsoever- just a series of puzzles. You have basically no agency- there are about five binary choices throughout the game (more on that later), and you often have some flexibility in what order you tackle puzzles, but that&apos;s it. 

The puzzles are... OK. Mostly quite easy, the odd genuinely interesting, a few a bit obtuse, but nothing terrible. They are a bit repetitive, especially the map puzzles, which are of only a few basic types. The game is quite short- about 5 hours. 

The plant identifying puzzles are marred by interface/mechanical issues, especially as you progress through the game and collect more and more plants and pages in your plant guide. One odd thing is that even when your customers can&apos;t name the plant they need (they just describe it, or explain what they need it to do), the game usually tells you anyway. This feels off, but it&apos;s actually necessary because there&apos;s no way to search the plant guide by anything except plant name (and if the plant has multiple names, you can only find it under one of them). So when you do (as you do occasionally) have to hunt for a specific effect, or a name that isn&apos;t the primary one, there&apos;s no alternative but to look through the whole guide.

There&apos;s also no way to find a plant in your greenhouse that you&apos;ve already identified besides remembering where you put it, or hunting through all the labels you&apos;ve attached manually. This quickly becomes a chore. 

The story is... again... OK. The writing is competent, but the story isn&apos;t that interesting, and the characters don&apos;t feel very real. 

Oh, and... this is a puzzle game. As such, it has zero replay value, of course. Yet despite this, the game has multiple endings (thanks to those binary choices). Since there&apos;s no way you&apos;ve ever want to play through it a second time, this doesn&apos;t really add anything. </review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>9794c738-81e6-11ec-b27c-fa163e9aa6fa</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:rating>
        <review:title>Disappointing puzzle game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/97960e52-b705-11ea-a0e6-fa163e62c76a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/97960e52-b705-11ea-a0e6-fa163e62c76a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2020-06-25T20:02:03</review:date>
        <review:description>Im allgemeinen ist das Spiel sehr interessant. Die Grafik ist etwas ungewöhnlich, aber passt gut zum Spiel. In ein oder zwei Fällen war es etwas problematisch für mich zu erkennen was passiert.

Man schaut sich einzelne Szenen an die sich auf dem Schiff abgespielt haben und identifiziert anhand dessen was man dort sieht und hört, wer wer ist und was mit ihnen passiert ist. Einige sind relativ einfach zu identifizieren und sind schnell geklärt. Für andere muss man schon genau aufpassen und auf kleinste Details achten.
Ich muss zugeben das ich in ein paar Fällen einfach die Möglichkeiten ausprobiert habe, bis mir das Spiel alles verifiziert hat. Das war entweder weil ich die Namen von 2 oder 3 Charakteren eingrenzen konnte, aber nicht genau identifizieren konnte wer von ihnen nun wer ist.

Bei den Schicksalen dagegen muss ich leider sagen, dass ein paar von ihnen nicht eindeutig sind. 
z.B.
Ein Charakter wurde von einer Kanone zerquetscht, diese Kanone wurde allerdings von einer Bestie gehalten. Für mich war hier nicht klar, ob ich Bestie oder Kanone eingeben soll.
2 Charaktere standen hintereinander vor einer Kanone und starben als sie losging. Erwartet wurde das einer explodiert ist und der andere durch die Kanone zerfetzt wurde. Für mich ist immernoch nicht klar warum nicht beide das gleiche haben.
Ein Fall denke ich kam durch eine schlechte Übersetzung, in einem Dialog wurde gesagt die Person wurde zerfetzt, gemeint war aber zerrissen.

Für das Buch hätte ich mir ein paar Filteroptionen gewünscht, z.B. in den Skizzen unidentifizierte Personen hervorheben.
Wenn man den Täter auswählen soll, wäre es gut wenn man die Option hätte sie mit der Person in den Skizzen zu verlinken.
Generell direkt im Spiel ein paar mehr Optionen für Notizen wären hilfreich gewesen.
Die Lesezeichen die man sich setzen konnte für Personen waren dagegen ziemlich nutzlos.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>97960e52-b705-11ea-a0e6-fa163e62c76a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>Interessantes Rätsel</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/98b2b8bc-945c-11e8-b763-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/98b2b8bc-945c-11e8-b763-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2011-03-25T12:04:42</review:date>
        <review:description>It&apos;s just your old-school fps except the keys to open the doors are not randomly hidden in the map and some npc can &quot;talk&quot; thus engaging you more in a story that does not exist.

I had some lust for this game in 1999 but lacked the money to buy it. Thanks to GOG, I played it 12 years later.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>98b2b8bc-945c-11e8-b763-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</review:rating>
        <review:title>Good old game, not so memorable</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/98d2a7b2-c585-11e8-8dcd-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/98d2a7b2-c585-11e8-8dcd-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2017-02-09T07:23:43</review:date>
        <review:description>When I was about 10, I tried to write a horror story. It made no sense, things just kept happening and changing out of the blue, but I was proud of it at the time and claimed that other people &quot;don&apos;t get it&quot;.

This game reminds me of that. Things keep happening here with no rhyme or reason. You&apos;re walking (painfully slowly by the way) down the hall, then there&apos;s a CUT and you&apos;re in an office getting a document, CUT you&apos;re in a house, CUT you get punched in the face, CUT and there&apos;s a dead bird, CUT and it goes all the hell with random stuff that goes on. It gets truly ridiculous very quickly. The first hour or so of the game is truly abysmal in this manner, and when it finally begins to seem somewhat interesting with one quick montage, it doubles down on the ridiculous drug trip and the last 10 minutes or so of the game are just complete and utter nonsense. And then it just cuts to credits.

The other parts of the game are just as inconsistent. The graphics is fairly interesting in an artsy low-budget way (although even this has issues, such as short arms and truly scary hands), but the colorful cartoony art style is a really poor fit for a detective mystery. So is the music most of the time, while at least that is great on its own. There&apos;s no dialog or speech in the game, not even any voices, everyone just silently emotes, which means there&apos;s little to no chance to provide much characterization or story (the rough visual style doesn&apos;t help either). This actually fits the last moments of the game well where you suddenly switch between characters and such, but not the majority of it.

This thing simply makes no sense. Maybe I &quot;don&apos;t get it&quot;, but more likely this is a typical example of being artsy just for the sake of being artsy. It wouldn&apos;t be an issue if this was an experiment given away for free, but all the descriptions of this thing are a complete lie. Thus, my money and even that one hour or so it took to &quot;play&quot; through were completely wasted.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">52</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>98d2a7b2-c585-11e8-8dcd-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</review:rating>
        <review:title>A selection of non-sequiturs</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">278</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/98e13d84-93ff-11e8-9aa3-00163e97122a -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/98e13d84-93ff-11e8-9aa3-00163e97122a">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl#Review"/>
        <review:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2009-09-25T06:05:07</review:date>
        <review:description>I was first introduced to space sims via the Tie-Fighter and X-Wing series. Freespace is definitely reminiscent of those great games. A joystick is recommended for that, &quot;in the cockpit&quot; feeling, but a sensitive mouse works for the most part. Some of the level can be difficult at times, but the player can also adjust the difficulty on the fly and restarting a mission is a very quick task with almost no loading time on my Lenovo T400 laptop. The enemy ships are well designed and distinct so that you can easily tell what spacecraft you are fighting. The story doesn&apos;t have much in the way of surprises, but it does put you in the mood to kick some aliens out of our galaxy.  Missions also require that you make sure your squads are placed strategically so it isn&apos;t just a matter of fighting.  Shield distribution and power, engine power, and weapon power are configurable in the heat of battle while loadouts, squads, and craft types are modifiable before most missions. There is a good mix of escort, inspection, fighter vs. fighter, and bomber vs. capital missions.</review:description>
        <review:downvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</review:downvotes>
        <review:id>98e13d84-93ff-11e8-9aa3-00163e97122a</review:id>
        <review:rating rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</review:rating>
        <review:title>A very enjoyable game</review:title>
        <review:upvotes rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</review:upvotes>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/981c013a-92e1-11ea-9b49-fa163e93294d -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/naymoll/ontologies/2022/3/games.owl/review/981c013a-92e1-11ea-9b49-fa163e93294d">
